                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Research the mechanism of action of dapsone.\n2. Identify the indications for which dapsone is used.\n3. Determine the mechanism of action of dapsone for each indication.\n4. Choose the option that matches the mechanism of action for all indications.\n\nAnswer: D) Competition with PABA in folic acid synthesis.\n\nExplanation:\n1. Dapsone is a sulfone antibiotic that has both antibacterial and antifungal properties. It is also used for its immunomodulatory actions.\n2. Dapsone is used for the treatment of bacterial and fungal infections, such as leprosy, dermatitis herpetiformis, and Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia. It is also used for its immunomodulatory actions in conditions such as lupus erythematosus and dermatomyositis.\n3. The mechanism of action of dapsone for bacterial and fungal infections is through the inhibition of dihydropteroate synthase, an enzyme involved in the synthesis of folic acid. Dapsone competes with para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) for binding to dihydropteroate synthase, which ultimately leads to the inhibition of folic acid synthesis and subsequent inhibition of bacterial and fungal growth. For its immunomodulatory actions, dapsone is thought to inhibit the production of reactive oxygen species and cytokines, which are involved in the inflammatory response.\n4. The option that matches the mechanism of action for all indications is D) Competition with PABA in folic acid synthesis.",
