                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "How does dapsone work to treat bacterial and fungal infections and have immunomodulatory actions?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- Dapsone is a medication used to treat bacterial and fungal infections, as well as for immunomodulatory actions.\n- To understand how it works, we need to look at its mechanism of action.\n- Option A, inhibition of cell wall synthesis, is a mechanism used by antibiotics such as penicillin and cephalosporins to treat bacterial infections. However, dapsone is not an antibiotic that targets cell wall synthesis.\n- Option B, inhibition of ergosterol in cell membranes, is a mechanism used by antifungal medications such as azoles and polyenes to treat fungal infections. Dapsone is known to have some antifungal activity, so this option is a possibility.\n- Option C, inhibition of protein synthesis, is a mechanism used by antibiotics such as macrolides and tetracyclines to treat bacterial infections. However, dapsone is not an antibiotic that targets protein synthesis.\n- Option D, competition with PABA in folic acid synthesis, is the mechanism used by dapsone to treat bacterial infections and have immunomodulatory actions. Dapsone is a sulfone drug that competes with para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) for the enzyme dihydropteroate synthase, which is involved in the synthesis of folic acid. By inhibiting folic acid synthesis, dapsone can kill bacteria and modulate the immune system.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Competition with PABA in folic acid synthesis.",
