                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Read the question and identify the topic.\n\nThe question is asking about the mechanism of dapsone for the treatment of bacterial and fungal infections, as well as for immunomodulatory actions.\n\nStep 2: Recall the mechanism of action of dapsone.\n\nDapsone is known to inhibit the synthesis of dihydrofolic acid by competing with para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) for the active site of dihydropteroate synthase, an enzyme involved in folic acid synthesis. This leads to a decrease in the production of nucleic acids, which are essential for the growth and replication of bacteria and fungi.\n\nDapsone also has immunomodulatory actions, which are thought to be due to its ability to inhibit the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS) by neutrophils and other immune cells.\n\nStep 3: Match the mechanism of action with the options given.\n\nA) Inhibition of cell wall synthesis - This mechanism is associated with antibiotics such as penicillins and cephalosporins, which target the cell wall of bacteria. Dapsone does not inhibit cell wall synthesis.\n\nB) Inhibition of ergosterol in cell membranes - This mechanism is associated with antifungal drugs such as azoles and polyenes, which target the synthesis or function of ergosterol, a key component of fungal cell membranes. Dapsone does not inhibit ergosterol synthesis.\n\nC) Inhibition of protein synthesis - This mechanism is associated with antibiotics such as macrolides and tetracyclines, which target the ribosomes of bacteria and inhibit protein synthesis. Dapsone does not inhibit protein synthesis.\n\nD) Competition with PABA in folic acid synthesis - This mechanism is associated with dapsone, which competes with PABA for the active site of dihydropteroate synthase and inhibits folic acid synthesis. This leads to a decrease in the production of nucleic acids, which are essential for the growth and replication of bacteria and fungi. This mechanism is consistent with the indications for which dapsone is used.\n\nStep 4: Select the correct answer.\n\nThe correct answer is D) Competition with PABA in folic acid synthesis.",
