                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Dapsone is a sulfone antibiotic that has both antimicrobial and anti-inflammatory properties. It is used for the treatment of various bacterial and fungal infections, as well as for its immunomodulatory actions. To determine the mechanism of dapsone for these indications, we need to consider the following options:\n\nA) Inhibition of cell wall synthesis\nB) Inhibition of ergosterol in cell membranes\nC) Inhibition of protein synthesis\nD) Competition with PABA in folic acid synthesis\n\nSub-questions:\n1) Does dapsone inhibit cell wall synthesis?\n2) Does dapsone inhibit ergosterol in cell membranes?\n3) Does dapsone inhibit protein synthesis?\n4) Does dapsone compete with PABA in folic acid synthesis?\n\nAnswers to sub-questions:\n1) Dapsone does not inhibit cell wall synthesis. This mechanism is associated with antibiotics like penicillins and cephalosporins.\n2) Dapsone does not inhibit ergosterol in cell membranes. This mechanism is associated with antifungal agents like azoles and polyenes.\n3) Dapsone does not inhibit protein synthesis. This mechanism is associated with antibiotics like tetracyclines and macrolides.\n4) Dapsone does compete with PABA in folic acid synthesis. It is a structural analog of PABA and acts as a competitive inhibitor of dihydropteroate synthase, an enzyme involved in the synthesis of folic acid. By inhibiting this enzyme, dapsone prevents the formation of folic acid, which is essential for the growth and reproduction of bacteria and some fungi.\n\nBased on the answers to the sub-questions, the mechanism of dapsone for its antimicrobial and immunomodulatory indications is:\n\nD) Competition with PABA in folic acid synthesis.",
