                        "cot": "\nThe best test for assessment of intestinal malabsorption is A) Fecal fat estimation.\n\nFecal fat estimation is the most reliable test for assessing intestinal malabsorption because it measures the amount of fat that is not absorbed in the small intestine. This test is used to diagnose conditions such as celiac disease, cystic fibrosis, and pancreatic insufficiency, which can all lead to malabsorption.\n\nSerum lactose levels measure the amount of lactose in the blood, which can be used to diagnose lactose intolerance, but it is not as reliable for assessing intestinal malabsorption as fecal fat estimation.\n\nSerum amylase levels measure the amount of amylase in the blood, which can be used to diagnose pancreatic insufficiency, but it is not as reliable for assessing intestinal malabsorption as fecal fat estimation.\n\nThe NBT-PABA test measures the amount of vitamin B12 in the blood, which can be used to diagnose pernicious anemia, but it is not as reliable for assessing intestinal malabsorption as fecal fat estimation.",
