                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The Bezold-Jarisch reflex is a physiological response that occurs when certain chemicals stimulate specific receptors in the heart. This reflex results in a decrease in heart rate, blood pressure, and cardiac output. It is essential to understand the chemical mediator responsible for this reflex to answer the question correctly.\n\nHelpful knowledge and important additional facts:\n- The Bezold-Jarisch reflex is triggered by the stimulation of specific receptors called serotonin (5-HT) receptors, particularly the 5-HT3 subtype.\n- Serotonin is a neurotransmitter that plays a crucial role in various physiological processes, including mood regulation, appetite, and cardiovascular function.\n- Angiotensin, prostaglandin, and histamine are other chemical mediators involved in different physiological processes, but they are not directly responsible for the Bezold-Jarisch reflex.\n\nSub-questions:\n1) Which chemical mediator is responsible for stimulating the 5-HT3 receptors in the Bezold-Jarisch reflex?\nAnswer: Serotonin\n\n2) Are angiotensin, prostaglandin, or histamine involved in the Bezold-Jarisch reflex?\nAnswer: No, these chemical mediators are not directly responsible for the Bezold-Jarisch reflex.\n\nBased on the information and answers to the sub-questions, the Bezold-Jarisch reflex is mediated by:\n\nA) Serotonin",
