                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what the Golgi tendon organ is?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the Golgi tendon organ is a sensory receptor located in the tendons of muscles. It's responsible for detecting changes in muscle tension.\n\nStudent: What do you mean by efferent neurons?\n\nProfessor: Efferent neurons are motor neurons that carry signals from the central nervous system to the muscles.\n\nStudent: So, what does the Golgi tendon organ relay to the efferent neurons?\n\nProfessor: The Golgi tendon organ continuously relays information about the tension in the muscle to the efferent neurons.\n\nStudent: Why is this important during voluntary movements?\n\nProfessor: It's important because it helps to regulate the amount of force that's applied to the muscle during movement. If the tension in the muscle gets too high, the Golgi tendon organ will signal the efferent neurons to reduce the force being applied.\n\nStudent: Does the Golgi tendon organ also detect changes in muscle length?\n\nProfessor: Yes, it does. It can detect changes in muscle length before and after movement.\n\nStudent: What about changes in joint angle?\n\nProfessor: No, the Golgi tendon organ doesn't detect changes in joint angle. That's the job of other sensory receptors, such as the muscle spindle.",
