                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The question is asking for the gene commonly indicated in congenital cataract. To answer this question, we need to consider each of the options provided and determine which one is most commonly associated with congenital cataract.\n\nOption A is PAX-6. PAX-6 is a gene that is involved in the development of the eye. Mutations in this gene have been associated with a variety of eye disorders, including congenital cataract. Therefore, option A is a possible answer to the question.\n\nOption B is CRYGS-3. CRYGS-3 is a gene that codes for a protein called gamma-crystallin. Gamma-crystallin is one of the proteins that make up the lens of the eye. Mutations in this gene have been associated with congenital cataract. Therefore, option B is a possible answer to the question.\n\nOption C is LMX-IB. LMX-IB is a gene that is involved in the development of the midbrain and hindbrain. It is not directly involved in the development of the eye or the lens. Therefore, option C is unlikely to be the correct answer to the question.\n\nOption D is PITX-3. PITX-3 is a gene that is involved in the development of the eye and the lens. Mutations in this gene have been associated with a variety of eye disorders, including congenital cataract. Therefore, option D is a possible answer to the question.\n\nBased on the information provided, options A, B, and D are all possible answers to the question. However, if we consider which gene is most commonly associated with congenital cataract, we can eliminate options A and D. Studies have shown that mutations in CRYGS-3 are the most common cause of congenital cataract. Therefore, the correct answer to the question is B) CRYGS-3.",
