                        "cot": "Question: Which gene is commonly associated with congenital cataracts?\nOptions:\nA) PAX-6\nB) CRYGS-3\nC) LMX-IB\nD) PITX-3\n\nStep 1: Understand the question\nThe question is asking us to identify the gene that is most commonly linked to the development of congenital cataracts, which are cataracts present at birth or shortly after.\n\nStep 2: Analyze the options\nA) PAX-6: This gene is involved in eye and brain development, but it is more commonly associated with aniridia, a condition characterized by the absence of the colored part of the eye (iris).\nB) CRYGS-3: This option seems to be a combination of two different genes. CRYGS is a gene that encodes a protein involved in maintaining the transparency of the eye lens, but it is not the most commonly associated gene with congenital cataracts. The \"-3\" part does not seem to be relevant.\nC) LMX-IB: This gene is involved in limb development and is associated with Nail-Patella Syndrome, which affects the nails, knees, elbows, and pelvis. It is not commonly associated with congenital cataracts.\nD) PITX-3: This gene is involved in eye development, and mutations in this gene have been found to cause congenital cataracts, specifically anterior polar cataracts.\n\nStep 3: Determine the correct answer\nBased on the analysis of the options, the gene most commonly associated with congenital cataracts is PITX-3 (option D).",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "361e65d1-3a03-4365-8d7c-6a97ecf63aab",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) PITX-3.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "D",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
