                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I'm having trouble understanding which gene is commonly indicated in congenital cataract. Can you help me work through this?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's go through the options one by one. First, let's look at option A, PAX-6. Do you know what PAX-6 is responsible for?\n\nStudent: Yes, PAX-6 is a transcription factor that plays a role in the development of the eye, as well as the central nervous system.\n\nProfessor: That's correct. Now, let's move on to option B, CRYGS-3. Are you familiar with this gene?\n\nStudent: I'm not sure. I think it has something to do with crystallins, but I don't know its specific function.\n\nProfessor: You're on the right track. CRYGS-3 is a gene that encodes for a protein called gamma-crystallin S, which is a member of the crystallin family. Crystallins are structural proteins found in the lens of the eye and are important for maintaining lens transparency. Now, let's look at option C, LMX-1B. Do you know what this gene does?\n\nStudent: LMX-1B is a transcription factor involved in limb and eye development, right?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. Finally, let's discuss option D, PITX-3. Are you familiar with this gene?\n\nStudent: I think PITX-3 is another transcription factor, but I'm not sure what it's responsible for.\n\nProfessor: You're right, PITX-3 is a transcription factor, and it plays a crucial role in the development of the lens and the anterior segment of the eye. Mutations in this gene have been associated with congenital cataracts and other ocular disorders. So, based on our discussion, which gene do you think is commonly indicated in congenital cataract?\n\nStudent: I think the correct answer is D, PITX-3, since it plays a crucial role in lens development and has been associated with congenital cataracts.\n\nProfessor: That's correct! Great job working through the options and understanding the functions of each gene. Remember, understanding the role of each gene in development and disease can help you answer questions like this more easily in the future.\n\nStudent: Thank you, Professor! I appreciate your help in breaking down the question and helping me understand the reasoning behind the correct answer.",
