                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) CVP (Central Venous Pressure) - This is a measurement of the pressure in the large veins returning blood to the heart. It can be a useful guide for fluid management in critically ill patients, but it is not the only factor to consider.\n\nB) Urine output - Monitoring urine output can be a helpful indicator of kidney function and overall perfusion, but it is not the only factor to consider in resuscitation management.\n\nC) Blood pressure - Maintaining adequate blood pressure is essential for perfusion of vital organs, but it is not the only factor to consider in resuscitation management.\n\nD) Saturation of Oxygen - Ensuring adequate oxygen delivery to tissues is important in resuscitation, but it is not the only factor to consider.\n\nIn conclusion, there is no single \"best\" guide for the management of resuscitation, as it depends on the individual patient's condition and needs. A combination of factors, including CVP, urine output, blood pressure, and oxygen saturation, should be considered and monitored during resuscitation to ensure optimal outcomes.",
                        "answers": [
