                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To determine which type of natural disaster causes the maximum number of deaths, we need to first understand the different categories of natural disasters and then analyze the historical data on fatalities caused by each type.\n\nHelpful knowledge and important additional facts:\n- Hydrological disasters are related to water, such as floods and landslides.\n- Meteorological disasters are related to weather and climate, such as storms, heatwaves, and cold waves.\n- Geological disasters are related to the Earth's structure, such as earthquakes and volcanic eruptions.\n- Fires are a separate category, including wildfires and other large-scale fires.\n\nSub-questions:\n1) What are the historical death tolls for each type of natural disaster?\n2) Which type of natural disaster has the highest average death toll per event?\n3) Which type of natural disaster has the highest cumulative death toll?\n\nAnswers to sub-questions:\n1) Historical death tolls for each type of natural disaster (approximate numbers based on available data):\n   - Hydrological: Over 5.4 million deaths since 1900 (mainly due to floods and landslides)\n   - Meteorological: Over 3.5 million deaths since 1900 (mainly due to storms, heatwaves, and cold waves)\n   - Geological: Over 2.3 million deaths since 1900 (mainly due to earthquakes and volcanic eruptions)\n   - Fires: Over 100,000 deaths since 1900 (mainly due to wildfires and other large-scale fires)\n\n2) The highest average death toll per event:\n   - Hydrological disasters have a lower average death toll per event compared to geological disasters, as floods and landslides usually affect smaller areas.\n   - Meteorological disasters also have a lower average death toll per event compared to geological disasters, as storms and extreme temperatures usually affect larger areas but cause fewer deaths.\n   - Geological disasters have the highest average death toll per event, as earthquakes and volcanic eruptions can cause massive destruction and fatalities in a short period.\n   - Fires have a lower average death toll per event compared to geological disasters, as they usually affect smaller areas and can be controlled more effectively.\n\n3) The highest cumulative death toll:\n   - Hydrological disasters have the highest cumulative death toll, followed by meteorological, geological, and fires.\n\nAnswer to the original question:\nBased on the historical data and analysis, hydrological disasters (A) have caused the maximum number of deaths.",
