                    {
                        "id": "5347b1a0-02a4-407d-ae60-5380bc6e3206",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, let's first understand what each type of sampling method means:\n\nA) Systematic random sampling: In this method, every nth element from the population is selected. For example, if we have a list of 100 patients and we want to select 10, we can choose every 10th patient.\n\nB) Simple random sampling: In this method, each element in the population has an equal chance of being selected. For example, if we have a list of 100 patients and we want to select 10, we can use a random number generator to choose 10 patients.\n\nC) Stratified random sampling: In this method, the population is divided into subgroups (strata) based on certain characteristics, and then a random sample is taken from each subgroup. This ensures that the sample represents the different subgroups in the population.\n\nD) Cluster sampling: In this method, the population is divided into clusters, and then a random sample of clusters is selected. All elements within the selected clusters are included in the sample.\n\nNow, let's analyze the question: \"Which of the following is used for selecting patients with respect to potential factors that will affect the results?\"\n\nWe are looking for a sampling method that takes into account potential factors that may affect the results. This means we need a method that considers the different subgroups in the population based on these factors.\n\nThe only sampling method that does this is stratified random sampling (option C). In stratified random sampling, the population is divided into subgroups based on certain characteristics (potential factors), and then a random sample is taken from each subgroup. This ensures that the sample represents the different subgroups in the population and takes into account the potential factors that may affect the results.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Stratified random sampling.",
                        "answers": [
