                    {
                        "id": "b9df2e1b-fea8-4cd9-89ec-1937676cf879",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The question is asking us to identify the sampling method that is used for selecting patients while considering potential factors that may affect the results of a study or experiment.\n\nHelpful knowledge and important additional facts:\n- Sampling methods are techniques used to select a subset of individuals from a larger population for the purpose of conducting research or experiments.\n- The goal of using different sampling methods is to obtain a representative sample that accurately reflects the characteristics of the larger population.\n\nNow, let's briefly define each of the given sampling methods:\n\nA) Systematic random sampling: In this method, every nth individual from the population is selected. This method ensures equal representation of all individuals but does not account for potential factors that may affect the results.\n\nB) Simple random sampling: In this method, individuals are selected randomly from the population, ensuring that each individual has an equal chance of being selected. This method also does not account for potential factors that may affect the results.\n\nC) Stratified random sampling: In this method, the population is divided into strata (subgroups) based on certain characteristics or factors, and then a random sample is taken from each stratum. This method ensures that the sample is representative of the population with respect to the factors considered in the stratification.\n\nD) Cluster sampling: In this method, the population is divided into clusters (groups), and a random sample of clusters is selected. All individuals within the selected clusters are included in the sample. This method does not specifically account for potential factors that may affect the results.\n\nSub-questions:\n1) Which sampling method divides the population into subgroups based on certain characteristics or factors?\nAnswer: Stratified random sampling.\n\n2) Which sampling method ensures that the sample is representative of the population with respect to the factors considered in the stratification?\nAnswer: Stratified random sampling.\n\nBased on the answers to the sub-questions, we can conclude that the sampling method used for selecting patients with respect to potential factors that will affect the results is:\n\nC) Stratified random sampling.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "59c1e192-c943-46fd-9c31-1a0d763c3e3b",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) Stratified random sampling.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
                                "correct_answer": true
