                    {
                        "id": "dc0c0824-48cd-4e67-9c4e-58ac50e50510",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-10",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nStep 1: Understand the question.\nThe question is asking which of the following sampling methods is used for selecting patients with respect to potential factors that will affect the results.\n\nStep 2: Understand the options.\nA) Systematic random sampling: This method involves selecting a sample from a population by randomly selecting a starting point and then selecting every nth element from the population.\nB) Simple random sampling: This method involves randomly selecting a sample from a population without any regard to any potential factors that may affect the results.\nC) Stratified random sampling: This method involves dividing the population into subgroups (strata) based on potential factors that may affect the results, and then randomly selecting a sample from each stratum.\nD) Cluster sampling: This method involves dividing the population into clusters and then randomly selecting a sample from each cluster.\n\nStep 3: Determine the answer.\nThe answer is C) Stratified random sampling, as this method involves dividing the population into subgroups (strata) based on potential factors that may affect the results, and then randomly selecting a sample from each stratum.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "3be86a93-b7fc-483a-8781-98cb04231f65",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": " C) Stratified random sampling.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/04/17 14:40:46",
                        "api_service": "openai",
                        "model": "{'name': 'text-davinci-003', 'temperature': 0, 'max_tokens': 512}",
                        "comment": "",
                        "annotations": []
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "55a82cf7-d36a-499e-be29-de528165a3db",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nThe correct answer is C) Stratified random sampling.\n\nStratified random sampling is a method of sampling that involves dividing a population into smaller groups, or strata, based on certain characteristics. These characteristics can include age, gender, race, income, etc. Once the population is divided into strata, a random sample is taken from each stratum. This ensures that the sample is representative of the population as a whole, and that potential factors that could affect the results are taken into account.\n\nSystematic random sampling is a method of sampling that involves selecting a random starting point and then selecting every nth element from the population. This method is used when the population is too large to sample in its entirety.\n\nSimple random sampling is a method of sampling that involves randomly selecting a sample from the population without taking any other factors into account.\n\nCluster sampling is a method of sampling that involves dividing the population into clusters and then randomly selecting a sample from each cluster. This method is used when it is not possible to obtain a complete list of the population.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "f9ffb4f1-c587-4a01-bf1b-9f8a11eb710d",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": " C) Stratified random sampling.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/04/17 14:40:55",
                        "api_service": "openai",
                        "model": "{'name': 'text-davinci-003', 'temperature': 0, 'max_tokens': 512}",
                        "comment": "",
                        "annotations": []
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "db14924d-6f5f-4b31-b45f-778a67d33169",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nQuestion rephrased: Which of the following sampling methods is used to select patients based on potential factors that will affect the results?\n\nAnswer: C) Stratified random sampling\n\nExplanation: Stratified random sampling is a sampling method used to select a sample from a population that is divided into subgroups or strata. This method is used to ensure that the sample accurately reflects the characteristics of the population. In this case, the potential factors that will affect the results can be used to divide the population into strata, and then a sample can be taken from each stratum. This ensures that the sample accurately reflects the characteristics of the population with respect to the potential factors that will affect the results.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "18863f2d-53fa-4fb2-b468-a34e8a035575",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
