                        "cot": "Answer: C) Stratified random sampling\n\nCritique: The answer is correct because stratified random sampling is a method used to ensure that specific subgroups within a population are adequately represented in the sample. This is particularly useful when there are potential factors that may affect the results, as it allows for better control and understanding of these factors.\n\nReconsidering other options:\nA) Systematic random sampling - This method involves selecting every nth individual from a population, which does not necessarily account for potential factors affecting the results.\nB) Simple random sampling - This method involves selecting individuals randomly from a population, without considering any potential factors that may affect the results.\nD) Cluster sampling - This method involves selecting groups or clusters of individuals, rather than individual participants, which may not adequately account for potential factors affecting the results.\n\nFinal answer: C) Stratified random sampling",
