                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What tests should not be done for a patient with altered sensorium in the emergency department?\n\nTo determine which tests should not be done for a patient with altered sensorium in the emergency department, we can consider each option and eliminate the one that is not necessary or appropriate:\n\nA) Complete blood counts - This test can help identify any abnormalities in the patient's blood, such as anemia or infection, which could be contributing to their altered sensorium. Therefore, this test should be done.\n\nB) Random blood sugar - This test can help identify if the patient's altered sensorium is due to hypoglycemia or hyperglycemia. Therefore, this test should be done.\n\nC) Lumbar puncture - This test involves inserting a needle into the patient's spinal canal to collect cerebrospinal fluid for analysis. It is typically done to diagnose infections or other conditions affecting the central nervous system. While it may be necessary in some cases of altered sensorium, it is not always required and can be risky. Therefore, this test may not be done.\n\nD) Non-contrast CT head - This test can help identify any structural abnormalities or bleeding in the brain that could be causing the patient's altered sensorium. Therefore, this test should be done.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C) Lumbar puncture should not be done for a patient with altered sensorium in the emergency department.",
