                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Hi Professor, can you explain the question again?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the question is asking which test should not be done when a patient comes to the emergency with altered sensorium.\n\nStudent: Okay, so what does altered sensorium mean?\n\nProfessor: Altered sensorium means a change in the patient's level of consciousness or awareness. They may be confused, disoriented, or have difficulty responding to questions.\n\nStudent: Got it. So, what are the options for tests that should not be done?\n\nProfessor: The options are A) Complete blood counts, B) Random blood sugar, C) Lumbar puncture, and D) Non-contrast CT head.\n\nStudent: Okay, so why would we not do a complete blood count?\n\nProfessor: A complete blood count is a test that measures the number of different types of cells in the blood. While it can be helpful in diagnosing certain conditions, it is not typically used to evaluate altered sensorium.\n\nStudent: And what about a random blood sugar?\n\nProfessor: A random blood sugar test measures the amount of glucose in the blood at a given time. While it can be helpful in diagnosing conditions like diabetes, it is not typically used to evaluate altered sensorium.\n\nStudent: I see. What about a lumbar puncture?\n\nProfessor: A lumbar puncture is a procedure where a needle is inserted into the lower back to collect cerebrospinal fluid for testing. It can be helpful in diagnosing conditions like meningitis, but it is not typically done in the emergency department for patients with altered sensorium.\n\nStudent: Okay, and what about a non-contrast CT head?\n\nProfessor: A non-contrast CT head is a type of imaging test that uses X-rays to create detailed images of the brain. It is often done in the emergency department for patients with altered sensorium to look for signs of bleeding or other abnormalities in the brain.\n\nStudent: So, the answer is A) Complete blood counts?\n\nProfessor: No, the answer is actually C) Lumbar puncture. While the other tests may be helpful in certain situations, a lumbar puncture is not typically done in the emergency department for patients with altered sensorium.",
