                        "cot": "Step 1: Understand the question\nThe question is asking which of the listed tests should NOT be done for a patient presenting to the emergency with altered sensorium.\n\nStep 2: Analyze each option\nA) Complete blood counts: This test is important to check for any infections, anemia, or other blood-related issues that could be causing the altered sensorium. So, this test should be done.\n\nB) Random blood sugar: Altered sensorium can be caused by high or low blood sugar levels. Therefore, checking the patient's blood sugar is important. So, this test should be done.\n\nC) Lumbar puncture: This test is used to collect cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) to check for infections, such as meningitis or encephalitis, which can cause altered sensorium. However, a lumbar puncture should not be done before ruling out other potential causes, as it is an invasive procedure and can have complications. So, this test should not be done immediately.\n\nD) Non-contrast CT head: A CT scan of the head can help identify any structural abnormalities, such as a brain tumor, hemorrhage, or stroke, which could be causing the altered sensorium. So, this test should be done.\n\nStep 3: Identify the correct answer\nThe correct answer is C) Lumbar puncture, as it should not be done immediately without ruling out other potential causes.",
