                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's rephrase the question to ensure we understand it correctly: A patient comes to the emergency room with altered consciousness. Which of the following tests should not be performed?\nA) Complete blood counts\nB) Random blood sugar\nC) Lumbar puncture\nD) Non-contrast CT head\n\nNow, let's work through this step by step, discussing our thoughts and reasoning:\n\nA) Complete blood counts: This test is important in evaluating a patient with altered sensorium, as it can help identify infections, anemia, or other blood-related issues that may be contributing to the patient's condition. Therefore, this test should be done.\n\nB) Random blood sugar: Checking blood sugar levels is crucial in a patient with altered consciousness, as both high and low blood sugar levels can cause changes in mental status. This test should be done.\n\nC) Lumbar puncture: A lumbar puncture is a procedure used to collect cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) to diagnose conditions such as meningitis or encephalitis, which can cause altered sensorium. However, it is an invasive procedure and should only be performed if there is a strong clinical suspicion of a central nervous system infection or other specific indications. It may not be necessary in all cases of altered sensorium, so this test might not need to be done.\n\nD) Non-contrast CT head: A non-contrast CT scan of the head can help identify structural abnormalities, such as bleeding, tumors, or swelling, which may be causing the patient's altered mental status. This test should be done.\n\nBased on our reasoning, the test that should not be performed in all cases of altered sensorium is:\n\nC) Lumbar puncture",
