                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Question: A patient presents to the emergency with altered sensorium. All of these tests should be done except:\nA) Complete blood counts\nB) Random blood sugar\nC) Lumbar puncture\nD) Non-contrast CT head\n\nHelpful knowledge and important additional facts:\n- Altered sensorium refers to a change in a person's level of consciousness or awareness, which can be caused by various factors such as infections, metabolic disturbances, neurological disorders, or drug intoxication.\n- Complete blood counts (CBC) can help identify infections, anemia, or other blood-related issues.\n- Random blood sugar (RBS) can help identify hyperglycemia or hypoglycemia, which can cause altered sensorium.\n- Lumbar puncture (LP) is a procedure used to collect cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) to diagnose infections like meningitis or encephalitis, which can cause altered sensorium.\n- Non-contrast CT head is an imaging study that can help identify structural abnormalities, such as tumors, hemorrhages, or strokes, which can cause altered sensorium.\n\nSub-questions:\n1) Are complete blood counts necessary for a patient with altered sensorium?\nYes, CBC can help identify potential causes of altered sensorium, such as infections or anemia.\n\n2) Is random blood sugar testing necessary for a patient with altered sensorium?\nYes, RBS can help identify potential causes of altered sensorium, such as hyperglycemia or hypoglycemia.\n\n3) Is a lumbar puncture necessary for a patient with altered sensorium?\nIt depends on the clinical context and other findings. If there is a suspicion of central nervous system infection or other conditions that can be diagnosed through CSF analysis, a lumbar puncture may be necessary. However, it may not be the first test to perform in all cases of altered sensorium.\n\n4) Is a non-contrast CT head necessary for a patient with altered sensorium?\nYes, a non-contrast CT head can help identify potential causes of altered sensorium, such as structural abnormalities, hemorrhages, or strokes.\n\nAnswer to the original question:\nAll of these tests should be done except:\nC) Lumbar puncture\n\nLumbar puncture may not be necessary in all cases of altered sensorium and should be considered based on the clinical context and other findings. The other tests (complete blood counts, random blood sugar, and non-contrast CT head) are generally useful in evaluating a patient with altered sensorium.",
                        "answers": [
