                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": " \n\nFirst, we need to understand what is meant by an altered sensorium. This refers to a change in a person's level of consciousness or awareness. This could manifest as confusion, drowsiness, or even coma. \n\nWith that in mind, we can start to eliminate some of the options. A complete blood count (CBC) would not be helpful in this case because it would not give any information about the patient's level of consciousness. The same is true for a random blood sugar test. \n\nA lumbar puncture, or spinal tap, could be helpful in this case because it could help to rule out meningitis, which can cause an altered sensorium. However, it is not the best option because it is invasive and carries some risks. \n\nA non-contrast CT head is the best option in this case because it can help to rule out a number of potential causes of an altered sensorium, including a brain tumor or bleed.",
                        "answers": [
