                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) Complete blood counts - This test should be done as it can help identify infections, anemia, or other hematological abnormalities that could contribute to altered sensorium.\n\nB) Random blood sugar - This test should be done as both high and low blood sugar levels can cause altered mental status.\n\nC) Lumbar puncture - This test may not be necessary in all cases of altered sensorium. It is typically done when there is a suspicion of meningitis, encephalitis, or other central nervous system infections. However, it should not be performed without first considering the patient's clinical presentation and other test results, as well as the potential risks associated with the procedure.\n\nD) Non-contrast CT head - This test should be done as it can help identify intracranial abnormalities such as hemorrhage, tumors, or edema that could be causing the altered mental status.\n\nTherefore, the answer is C) Lumbar puncture, as it may not be necessary in all cases of altered sensorium and should be considered based on the patient's clinical presentation and other test results.",
                        "answers": [
