                    {
                        "id": "3953bed3-1418-4a5a-87de-fa7f456b2cba",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "In this situation, an 8-year-old child has experienced dental trauma, resulting in the avulsion of their central incisor. The tooth has been contaminated with debris and it has been 20 minutes since the incident. The child has now arrived at the dental clinic with the avulsed tooth. We need to determine the best course of treatment.\n\nA) Clean root surface with saline and reimplant\nB) Clean and curette root surface and reimplant\nC) Clean root surface, do RCT (root canal treatment) and reimplant\nD) Scrubbing of root surface and reimplant\n\nFirst, let's consider the importance of time in this situation. The tooth has been out of the mouth for 20 minutes, which means that the periodontal ligament cells on the root surface may have started to dry out and become damaged. The sooner the tooth is reimplanted, the better the chances of successful healing and reattachment.\n\nOption A suggests cleaning the root surface with saline and reimplanting the tooth. This is a good first step, as it is important to remove any debris from the tooth before reimplantation. However, it may not be sufficient to ensure the best possible outcome.\n\nOption B suggests cleaning and curetting the root surface before reimplantation. Curetting involves scraping the root surface to remove any damaged periodontal ligament cells. This may help to promote better healing and reattachment of the tooth, but it is important to be gentle and not cause further damage to the root surface.\n\nOption C suggests cleaning the root surface, performing a root canal treatment (RCT), and then reimplanting the tooth. While RCT may be necessary in some cases of dental trauma, it is not typically performed immediately upon reimplantation. Instead, the tooth would be reimplanted and monitored for signs of infection or inflammation, at which point RCT may be considered.\n\nOption D suggests scrubbing the root surface before reimplantation. This may be too aggressive and could cause further damage to the periodontal ligament cells, reducing the chances of successful healing and reattachment.\n\nBased on this analysis, the best course of treatment in this situation would be Option B: Clean and gently curette the root surface, and then reimplant the tooth. This approach removes debris and damaged periodontal ligament cells while minimizing the risk of further damage to the root surface. Once the tooth is reimplanted, the dentist will likely splint",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "5397533a-91fe-495a-9a8c-4622c1a939a7",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) Clean and gently curette the root surface and reimplant.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "B",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
