                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nThe correct answer is B) Clean and curette root surface and reimplant.\n\nTo arrive at this answer, we must first consider the type of trauma the child experienced. Since the child's central incisor was avulsed, this means that the tooth was completely knocked out of the socket. This type of trauma is considered a severe injury, and the tooth is likely to be contaminated with debris.\n\nNext, we must consider the time frame. The child arrived at the dental clinic 20 minutes after the trauma occurred. This is important because the longer a tooth is out of the socket, the less likely it is to survive reimplantation.\n\nGiven the severity of the trauma and the time frame, the best course of action is to clean and curette the root surface of the tooth and then reimplant it. Cleaning and curetting the root surface will remove any debris that may have contaminated the tooth, and this will increase the chances of successful reimplantation. The other options (A, C, and D) are not recommended because they do not address the contamination issue.",
