                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, it is important to note that time is of the essence when dealing with an avulsed tooth. The longer the tooth is out of the socket, the lower the chances of successful reimplantation.\n\nIn this case, the child has already waited for 20 minutes before reaching the dental clinic. Therefore, the first step would be to assess the condition of the tooth and the socket.\n\nSince the tooth has been contaminated with debris, it is important to clean the root surface before reimplantation. However, scrubbing the root surface can damage the delicate periodontal ligament and reduce the chances of successful reimplantation.\n\nTherefore, options A and D can be ruled out.\n\nOption C suggests doing a root canal treatment before reimplantation. However, this is not necessary in all cases of avulsion. If the tooth is immature, a root canal treatment may not be required.\n\nTherefore, the best option in this case would be to clean and curette the root surface before reimplantation. This would help remove any debris and reduce the risk of infection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) Clean and curette root surface and reimplant.",
                        "answers": [
