                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what the Statistical Q test is used for?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the Statistical Q test is used for a few things. Which one would you like me to explain?\n\nStudent: Can you explain what it means to compare the proportion of means of two groups?\n\nProfessor: Of course. Comparing the proportion of means of two groups means that we are looking at the average value of a certain variable in two different groups and seeing if there is a significant difference between them.\n\nStudent: How does the Q test determine outliers?\n\nProfessor: The Q test determines outliers by looking at the difference between adjacent values in a dataset. If the difference is greater than a certain threshold, then that value is considered an outlier.\n\nStudent: Can you explain how the Q test determines normality distribution?\n\nProfessor: Yes, the Q test determines normality distribution by comparing the observed distribution of a dataset to the expected distribution of a normal distribution. If the observed distribution is significantly different from the expected distribution, then the dataset is not normally distributed.\n\nStudent: And finally, can you explain how the Q test compares the proportion of means of more than two groups?\n\nProfessor: Sure, when comparing the proportion of means of more than two groups, the Q test looks at the variance between the groups and compares it to the variance within the groups. If the variance between the groups is significantly greater than the variance within the groups, then there is a significant difference between the means of the groups.",
                        "answers": [
