                    {
                        "id": "4d024886-6402-4eab-9f15-efda5e35783c",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to understand what lactic acidosis is and how it is related to Metformin. Lactic acidosis is a medical condition characterized by the buildup of lactate in the body, which results in an excessively low pH in the bloodstream. Metformin is a medication used to treat type 2 diabetes, and one of its rare but severe side effects is lactic acidosis.\n\nNow, let's analyze each factor and determine if it increases the risk of lactic acidosis:\n\nA) Advanced age: Older adults are more likely to have reduced kidney function, which can affect the clearance of Metformin from the body, leading to an increased risk of lactic acidosis. Additionally, older adults may have other health conditions that can contribute to the development of lactic acidosis.\n\nB) Smoking: Smoking can cause various health issues, but it is not directly linked to an increased risk of lactic acidosis in patients taking Metformin. However, smoking can exacerbate other risk factors, such as cardiovascular disease and reduced kidney function, which can indirectly increase the risk of lactic acidosis.\n\nC) Liver dysfunction: The liver plays a crucial role in lactate metabolism and clearance. Liver dysfunction can impair this process, leading to an increased risk of lactic acidosis, especially in patients taking Metformin.\n\nD) Renal failure: Kidney function is essential for the clearance of Metformin from the body. Renal failure can lead to the accumulation of Metformin, increasing the risk of lactic acidosis.\n\nBased on this analysis, the factor that does not directly increase the risk of lactic acidosis in patients taking Metformin is:\n\nB) Smoking",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "1d88de8f-d966-498a-8747-c650bec59089",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) Smoking.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "B",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/04/13 01:13:55",
