                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's rephrase the question to ensure we understand it correctly: Which of the following routes of drug administration does NOT bypass hepatic first-pass metabolism?\n\nNow, let's work this out step by step by spelling out our thoughts and reasoning:\n\nHepatic first-pass metabolism refers to the process where a drug is metabolized in the liver before it reaches systemic circulation. Bypassing this process means that the drug will enter the bloodstream without being metabolized by the liver first.\n\nA) Oral: When a drug is administered orally, it is absorbed through the gastrointestinal tract and then transported to the liver via the portal vein. This means that the drug will undergo hepatic first-pass metabolism before entering systemic circulation. So, this route does not bypass hepatic first-pass metabolism.\n\nB) Intravenous: Intravenous administration involves injecting the drug directly into the bloodstream. This route bypasses the liver, allowing the drug to enter systemic circulation without undergoing hepatic first-pass metabolism.\n\nC) Sublingual: Sublingual administration involves placing the drug under the tongue, where it is absorbed directly into the bloodstream through the oral mucosa. This route bypasses the liver, allowing the drug to enter systemic circulation without undergoing hepatic first-pass metabolism.\n\nD) Intradermal: Intradermal administration involves injecting the drug into the dermis, the layer of skin just below the epidermis. This route allows the drug to be absorbed directly into the bloodstream, bypassing the liver and avoiding hepatic first-pass metabolism.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is A) Oral, as this is the route of drug administration that does NOT bypass hepatic first-pass metabolism.",
