                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: What is hepatic first pass metabolism?\nHepatic first pass metabolism refers to the process by which drugs taken orally are metabolized by the liver before they enter the systemic circulation. This can result in a significant reduction in the bioavailability of the drug.\n\nStep 2: Which routes of drug administration bypass hepatic first pass metabolism?\nIntravenous (IV) administration bypasses hepatic first pass metabolism because the drug is directly injected into the bloodstream. Sublingual administration also bypasses hepatic first pass metabolism because the drug is absorbed through the mucous membranes under the tongue and enters the systemic circulation directly. \n\nStep 3: Which routes of drug administration do not bypass hepatic first pass metabolism?\nOral administration does not bypass hepatic first pass metabolism because the drug must pass through the liver before entering the systemic circulation. Intradermal administration involves injecting the drug into the skin, which also does not bypass hepatic first pass metabolism.\n\nStep 4: What is the answer?\nThe answer is D) Intradermal, because it does not bypass hepatic first pass metabolism.",
                        "answers": [
