                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Hepatic first-pass metabolism refers to the liver's ability to metabolize a drug before it reaches systemic circulation. Bypassing this metabolism means that the drug will not be significantly metabolized by the liver before reaching the bloodstream.\n\nA) Oral: Drugs administered orally are absorbed through the gastrointestinal tract and then pass through the liver before entering systemic circulation. Therefore, hepatic first-pass metabolism is not bypassed in this route.\n\nB) Intravenous: Drugs administered intravenously are directly injected into the bloodstream, bypassing the liver and avoiding hepatic first-pass metabolism.\n\nC) Sublingual: Drugs administered sublingually are absorbed through the mucous membranes under the tongue and enter the bloodstream directly, bypassing the liver and avoiding hepatic first-pass metabolism.\n\nD) Intradermal: Drugs administered intradermally are injected into the skin and absorbed into the bloodstream, bypassing the liver and avoiding hepatic first-pass metabolism.\n\nSo, the correct answer is A) Oral, as this is the route of drug administration that does not bypass hepatic first-pass metabolism.",
