                    {
                        "id": "5cabf760-c2a5-4a0d-af72-08cc275f099d",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "B) Pyruvate carboxylase",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "fb73b3ae-f2f1-4444-acad-d080e86be748",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": " B, Pyruvate carboxylase.\n\nPyruvate carboxylase deficiency is an inherited disorder that causes a build-up of lactic acid in the body. This condition is characterized by episodic vomiting, seizures, and extreme tiredness (hypoglycemia).\n\nThe build-up of lactic acid can cause damage to the brain and nervous system.\n\nEarly and accurate diagnosis, along with a low-protein diet and medication, can help prevent serious complications.\n\nThe condition is inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern, which means the defective gene is located on an autosome, and two copies of the gene (one inherited from each parent) are needed to be symptomatic.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "B",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
