                    {
                        "id": "31bafa36-1ed9-4d36-b23c-3f125ba23073",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: \n- Raw eggs can contain bacteria such as Salmonella, which can cause food poisoning.\n- Hypoglycemia is a condition where blood sugar levels are abnormally low.\n- Enzymes are proteins that catalyze (speed up) chemical reactions in the body.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the role of each of the enzymes listed in the answer choices?\n2. How does a deficiency in each enzyme lead to hypoglycemia?\n3. Which enzyme is involved in the process of converting glycogen to glucose?\n4. Is there any connection between consuming raw eggs and enzyme deficiencies?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Phosphoenol pyruvate carboxykinase (PEPCK) is an enzyme involved in gluconeogenesis, the process of producing glucose from non-carbohydrate sources such as amino acids and fatty acids. Pyruvate carboxylase is also involved in gluconeogenesis, but specifically in the conversion of pyruvate to oxaloacetate. Glycogen phosphorylase is an enzyme involved in glycogenolysis, the breakdown of glycogen into glucose. Glucose 6 phosphatase is an enzyme involved in the final step of gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, the conversion of glucose 6-phosphate to glucose.\n2. A deficiency in PEPCK or pyruvate carboxylase would impair the body's ability to produce glucose from non-carbohydrate sources, leading to hypoglycemia. A deficiency in glycogen phosphorylase would impair the breakdown of glycogen into glucose, also leading to hypoglycemia. A deficiency in glucose 6 phosphatase would impair the final step of gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, preventing the release of glucose into the bloodstream and leading to hypoglycemia.\n3. Glycogen phosphorylase is involved in the breakdown of glycogen into glucose.\n4. Consuming raw eggs can lead to food poisoning, which can cause gastrointestinal symptoms such as nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. These symptoms can lead to dehydration and a decrease in blood sugar levels, but they are not directly related to enzyme deficiencies. However, consuming raw egg whites can also lead to a biotin deficiency, which can affect the activity of certain enzymes including pyruvate carboxylase. This can lead to impaired gluconeogenesis and hypoglycemia.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "8a1ad5f4-f7e0-45ad-9152-283705428ab6",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) Glycogen phosphorylase.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
