                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Understand the symptoms and their relation to enzyme deficiency\nThe patient presents with weakness, fatigue, and hypoglycemia. Hypoglycemia indicates low blood sugar levels, which means there is a problem with glucose production or release in the body.\n\nStep 2: Analyze the enzyme deficiencies and their effects on glucose metabolism\nA) Phosphoenol pyruvate carboxykinase (PEPCK) is involved in gluconeogenesis, the process of producing glucose from non-carbohydrate sources. A deficiency in this enzyme would impair glucose production, potentially leading to hypoglycemia.\nB) Pyruvate carboxylase is also involved in gluconeogenesis. A deficiency in this enzyme would have similar effects as PEPCK deficiency, impairing glucose production and potentially causing hypoglycemia.\nC) Glycogen phosphorylase is involved in glycogenolysis, the process of breaking down glycogen into glucose. A deficiency in this enzyme would impair the release of glucose from glycogen stores, potentially leading to hypoglycemia.\nD) Glucose 6 phosphatase is involved in the final step of both gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, converting glucose 6-phosphate to glucose. A deficiency in this enzyme would impair both glucose production and release, potentially causing hypoglycemia.\n\nStep 3: Consider the consumption of raw eggs\nConsuming raw eggs can lead to a biotin deficiency, as a protein called avidin in raw egg whites binds to biotin and prevents its absorption. Biotin is a cofactor for pyruvate carboxylase, so a deficiency in biotin would impair the function of this enzyme.\n\nStep 4: Conclusion\nConsidering the patient's symptoms, the consumption of raw eggs, and the role of biotin in pyruvate carboxylase function, the enzyme deficiency causing hypoglycemia in this patient is most likely B) Pyruvate carboxylase.",
                        "answers": [
