                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Understand the question\nThe question is asking which enzyme deficiency is causing hypoglycemia in a patient who has consumed raw eggs and is experiencing weakness, fatigue, and hypoglycemia.\n\nStep 2: Analyze the given information\nThe patient has consumed raw eggs and is experiencing hypoglycemia. The doctor gave him vitamin tablets, which indicates that the enzyme deficiency might be related to a vitamin deficiency.\n\nStep 3: Review the enzyme functions\nA) Phosphoenol pyruvate carboxykinase: This enzyme is involved in gluconeogenesis, the process of producing glucose from non-carbohydrate sources.\nB) Pyruvate carboxylase: This enzyme is also involved in gluconeogenesis, converting pyruvate to oxaloacetate. It requires biotin as a cofactor.\nC) Glycogen phosphorylase: This enzyme is involved in glycogenolysis, the process of breaking down glycogen into glucose.\nD) Glucose 6 phosphatase: This enzyme is involved in the final step of gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, converting glucose 6-phosphate to glucose.\n\nStep 4: Identify the enzyme deficiency related to vitamin deficiency\nSince the doctor gave the patient vitamin tablets, we can assume that the enzyme deficiency is related to a vitamin deficiency. Pyruvate carboxylase requires biotin as a cofactor, so a deficiency in this enzyme could be related to a vitamin deficiency.\n\nStep 5: Determine the enzyme deficiency causing hypoglycemia\nA deficiency in pyruvate carboxylase would impair gluconeogenesis, leading to hypoglycemia. Therefore, the enzyme deficiency causing hypoglycemia in this patient is pyruvate carboxylase.\n\nAnswer: B) Pyruvate carboxylase",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "c838667a-47a9-4239-86d3-f034bda52699",
