                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: An intraocular lens (IOL) is a synthetic lens that is implanted in the eye to replace the natural lens after cataract surgery. The IOL can last for many years, but may need to be replaced or removed in certain circumstances.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. Has the patient experienced any complications or issues with the IOL in the past 10 years?\n2. Is the IOL causing any discomfort or vision problems for the patient?\n3. Has the patient developed presbyopia or any other age-related vision changes?\n4. Is there any evidence of posterior capsule ossification?\n\nAnswer:\nTo determine what should be done with an IOL placed in a young male after 10 years, several sub-questions need to be answered. If the patient has experienced complications or issues with the IOL, it may need to be removed or replaced. If the IOL is causing discomfort or vision problems, it may need to be adjusted or removed. If the patient has developed presbyopia or other age-related vision changes, the IOL may need to be replaced with a different type of lens. Finally, if there is evidence of posterior capsule ossification, the IOL may need to be removed. Therefore, the answer to the original question depends on the specific circumstances of the patient's case.",
                        "answers": [
