                    {
                        "id": "f0506a2d-6f6f-42a0-92d8-20094f54ecf5",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to understand what an IOL is and the context of its usage.\n\nAn IOL, or intraocular lens, is an artificial lens that is implanted in the eye to replace the natural lens during cataract surgery. Cataract surgery is a common procedure to remove the clouded natural lens and replace it with an IOL to restore clear vision.\n\nNow let's analyze each option:\n\nA) IOL should be removed in case of Posterior Capsule ossification\nPosterior capsule ossification is a rare complication that can occur after cataract surgery. It involves the thickening and clouding of the posterior capsule, which can affect vision. However, the treatment for this condition is not to remove the IOL, but rather to perform a procedure called YAG laser capsulotomy, which creates an opening in the clouded capsule to restore clear vision.\n\nB) Never be removed\nIn general, IOLs are designed to be permanent and do not need to be removed or replaced unless there are complications or issues with the lens. However, this statement is too absolute, as there may be cases where removal or replacement is necessary.\n\nC) Remove when presbyopia sets in\nPresbyopia is an age-related condition in which the eye's lens loses its ability to focus on near objects. However, it is not a reason to remove or replace an IOL. Presbyopia can be managed with reading glasses or other corrective lenses.\n\nD) Should be changed after 10 years\nThere is no specific time frame for changing an IOL, as they are designed to be permanent. However, in some cases, an IOL may need to be replaced due to complications or issues with the lens, such as dislocation, damage, or incorrect power. This is not a routine procedure and is only done when necessary.\n\nBased on the analysis of each option, the most accurate answer is:\n\nB) Never be removed\n\nHowever, it is important to note that this statement should not be taken as an absolute rule, as there may be cases where removal or replacement of an IOL is necessary due to complications or issues with the lens.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "efc9973e-23f0-4320-b7bc-c4e0f14e35d0",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) Never be removed\n\nHowever, it is important to note that this statement should not be taken as an absolute rule, as there may be cases where removal or replacement of an IOL is necessary due to complications or issues with the lens.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "B",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
