                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's rephrase the question to ensure we understand it correctly: \"A young male has had an intraocular lens (IOL) placed. What should be done after 10 years?\"\nA) Remove the IOL if there is posterior capsule ossification\nB) The IOL should never be removed\nC) Remove the IOL when presbyopia occurs\nD) Replace the IOL after 10 years\n\nNow, let's work this out step by step, spelling out our thoughts and reasoning so anyone can verify them.\n\nIntraocular lenses (IOLs) are artificial lenses implanted in the eye to replace the natural lens, usually after cataract surgery. The goal of IOL implantation is to improve vision and reduce the need for glasses or contact lenses.\n\nA) Posterior capsule ossification is a rare complication that can occur after IOL implantation. It involves the formation of bone-like tissue in the posterior capsule, which can lead to decreased vision. If this occurs, the IOL may need to be removed or replaced. However, this is not a routine procedure that should be done after 10 years for all patients.\n\nB) In most cases, IOLs are designed to be permanent and do not need to be removed or replaced unless there is a specific complication or issue with the lens.\n\nC) Presbyopia is an age-related condition in which the eye's natural lens loses its ability to focus on near objects. IOLs can be designed to correct for presbyopia, so removing the IOL when presbyopia sets in would not be a standard recommendation.\n\nD) There is no specific time frame for when an IOL should be changed, as they are designed to be permanent. However, if there are complications or issues with the lens, it may need to be replaced.\n\nBased on this reasoning, the correct answer is:\nB) The IOL should never be removed, as it is designed to be a permanent solution unless there are specific complications or issues with the lens.",
                        "answers": [
