                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, it is important to understand what an IOL is. An IOL, or intraocular lens, is a small artificial lens that is implanted in the eye during cataract surgery to replace the natural lens that has become cloudy. \n\nNow, if an IOL has been placed in a young male, it is likely that he had cataract surgery at a young age. In this case, after 10 years, the IOL should not necessarily be removed unless there are complications or issues with the lens. \n\nOption A suggests that the IOL should be removed in case of posterior capsule ossification, which is a complication that can occur after cataract surgery where the capsule that holds the IOL becomes cloudy. In this case, the IOL may need to be removed or replaced. \n\nOption B, \"never be removed,\" is not necessarily true. While IOLs are designed to be a permanent replacement for the natural lens, they can sometimes cause issues or complications that require removal or replacement. \n\nOption C, \"remove when presbyopia sets in,\" is not necessarily true either. Presbyopia is a natural age-related condition where the eye's ability to focus on close objects decreases. While it may be necessary to adjust or replace the IOL as the patient ages and their vision changes, this is not necessarily related to presbyopia specifically. \n\nOption D, \"should be changed after 10 years,\" is also not necessarily true. While some IOLs may need to be replaced or adjusted over time, this is not a hard and fast rule. The decision to replace or adjust the IOL would depend on the individual patient's needs and any complications or issues that arise. \n\nIn summary, the correct answer is that the IOL should not necessarily be removed after 10 years unless there are complications or issues with the lens.",
