                    {
                        "id": "694196c9-f11d-4bce-842f-ec5ac7ad36f3",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's rephrase the question to ensure we understand it correctly: \"According to Piaget's theory, what is the age range for the preoperational stage?\"\n\nNow, let's work this out step by step, spelling out our thoughts and reasoning:\n\nJean Piaget was a Swiss psychologist who developed a theory of cognitive development in children. He divided cognitive development into four stages, one of which is the preoperational stage. The preoperational stage is the second stage in Piaget's theory, and it occurs after the sensorimotor stage.\n\nNow, let's look at the answer choices and determine which one corresponds to the preoperational stage:\n\nA) Birth to 18 months: This is the age range for the sensorimotor stage, which is the first stage in Piaget's theory.\n\nB) 12 years onwards: This is the age range for the formal operational stage, which is the fourth and final stage in Piaget's theory.\n\nC) 2-6 years: This is the age range for the preoperational stage, which is the second stage in Piaget's theory.\n\nD) 7-12 years: This is the age range for the concrete operational stage, which is the third stage in Piaget's theory.\n\nBased on our analysis, the correct answer is C) 2-6 years, as this is the age range for the preoperational stage according to Piaget's theory of cognitive development.",
                        "answers": [
