                    {
                        "id": "7f291edc-cf3c-40ce-a932-820bda3a4cc8",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "According to Piaget's theory of cognitive development, the period of the preoperational stage is:\n\nA) Birth to 18 months\nB) 12 years onwards.\nC) 2-6 years.\nD) 7-12 years.\n\nHelpful knowledge and important additional facts:\n- Jean Piaget was a Swiss psychologist who studied the cognitive development of children.\n- Piaget's theory consists of four stages: sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete operational, and formal operational.\n- Each stage represents a different age range and is characterized by specific cognitive abilities and limitations.\n\nSub-questions required to answer the original question:\n1) What are the age ranges and characteristics of each stage in Piaget's theory?\n\nAnswering sub-question 1:\n1) Sensorimotor stage (A): Birth to 18 months - In this stage, infants learn about the world through their senses and motor skills. They develop object permanence, which is the understanding that objects continue to exist even when they cannot be seen, heard, or touched.\n\n2) Preoperational stage (C): 2-6 years - In this stage, children begin to use symbols, such as words and images, to represent objects and ideas. They are still limited in their ability to think logically and have difficulty understanding the perspective of others (egocentrism).\n\n3) Concrete operational stage (D): 7-12 years - In this stage, children develop the ability to think logically about concrete events and objects. They can understand the concept of conservation, which is the understanding that certain properties of objects, such as volume or mass, remain the same even if their appearance changes.\n\n4) Formal operational stage (B): 12 years onwards - In this stage, adolescents develop the ability to think abstractly and hypothetically. They can consider multiple possibilities and use deductive reasoning to solve problems.\n\nAnswering the original question:\nAccording to Piaget's theory, the period of the preoperational stage is 2-6 years (C).",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "c0efe5c8-2a25-4ebe-a2e4-d70cdc2edd28",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) 2-6 years.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/04/14 18:23:28",
                        "api_service": "openai_chat",
