                        "cot": "What is the method used to assess post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- Post-operative vision effects refer to changes in vision that occur after cataract extraction surgery.\n- PCB stands for posterior chamber intraocular lens, which is a type of lens used in cataract surgery.\n- The question is asking about the method used to evaluate post-operative vision effects under PCB.\n- Option A, active surveillance, involves actively monitoring patients for adverse events. This may be useful in some cases, but it is not specific to evaluating post-operative vision effects after cataract surgery.\n- Option B, sentinel surveillance, is a type of surveillance used to detect early warning signs of a disease outbreak. This is not relevant to evaluating post-operative vision effects after cataract surgery.\n- Option C, passive surveillance, involves collecting data on adverse events that are reported by patients or healthcare providers. This may be useful in evaluating post-operative vision effects after cataract surgery, but it is not specific to PCB.\n- Option D, routine checkup of all operated cases, involves regularly monitoring patients who have undergone cataract surgery. This is likely the most appropriate method for evaluating post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Routine checkup of all operated cases.",
