                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To evaluate post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB, we need to consider the options given and determine which one is the most appropriate for this specific situation.\n\nA) Active surveillance: This involves actively seeking out and collecting data on the health status of a population. It is usually done for specific diseases or conditions and requires a lot of resources. While this could potentially be used to evaluate post-operative vision effects, it may not be the most efficient or practical method.\n\nB) Sentinel surveillance: This type of surveillance involves monitoring a select group of individuals or institutions (such as hospitals or clinics) that are considered representative of the larger population. This can provide valuable information on trends and changes in disease occurrence. However, this method may not be the most appropriate for evaluating post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery, as it may not capture all the relevant data needed for a comprehensive assessment.\n\nC) Passive surveillance: This involves collecting data on health events as they are reported by healthcare providers, without actively seeking out cases. This method relies on the routine reporting of health data and can be useful for monitoring trends in disease occurrence. However, it may not be the most appropriate method for evaluating post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery, as it may not capture all the relevant data needed for a comprehensive assessment.\n\nD) Routine checkup of all operated cases: This option involves conducting routine checkups on all patients who have undergone cataract extraction surgery under PCB. This would allow for a comprehensive assessment of post-operative vision effects, as it would capture data on all relevant cases. This method would be the most appropriate for evaluating post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB, as it would provide the most complete and accurate data.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Routine checkup of all operated cases.",
