                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) Active surveillance involves actively seeking out and collecting data on specific health events or outcomes. This may be appropriate for monitoring rare or severe conditions, but may not be the most efficient method for evaluating post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery.\n\nB) Sentinel surveillance involves monitoring a select group of healthcare facilities or providers to track specific health events or outcomes. This can provide valuable information on trends and patterns, but may not capture all cases of post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery.\n\nC) Passive surveillance involves collecting data on health events or outcomes as they are reported by healthcare providers or facilities. This can be a more efficient method for evaluating post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery, as it relies on existing data and does not require additional resources for data collection.\n\nD) Routine checkup of all operated cases would involve examining every patient who has undergone cataract extraction surgery to evaluate their post-operative vision effects. This would be a comprehensive approach, but may not be the most efficient or practical method for monitoring post-operative vision effects.\n\nBased on these considerations, the most appropriate method for evaluating post-operative vision effects after cataract extraction surgery under PCB would likely be passive surveillance (C).",
                        "answers": [
