                    {
                        "id": "9cfe8d4b-0f01-4b8b-ac1a-78944135296a",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the pulmonary compliance of a patient who inhales a tidal volume of 500 mL and has an intrapleural pressure of -4 cm of water before inspiration and -9 cm of water after inspiration?\n\nStep-by-step solution:\n1. The change in intrapleural pressure is -9 cm of water minus -4 cm of water, which equals -5 cm of water.\n2. The tidal volume is 500 mL, which is equivalent to 0.5 L.\n3. The formula for pulmonary compliance is change in volume divided by change in pressure. Therefore, pulmonary compliance = 0.5 L / (-5 cmH2O) = -0.1 L/cmH2O.\n4. However, the answer choices are in units of L/cm, so we need to convert cmH2O to cm. 1 cmH2O is equivalent to 0.098 kPa, and 1 kPa is equivalent to 10 cm of water. Therefore, -5 cmH2O is equivalent to -0.49 kPa, and pulmonary compliance = 0.1 L / (-0.49 kPa) = 0.204 L/cm.\n5. The closest answer choice is C) 0.2 L/cm.\n\nAnswer: C) 0.2 L/cm.",
                        "answers": [
