                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Understand the question\nThe question is asking which of the given options does not have optic atrophy as a symptom or result.\n\nStep 2: Define optic atrophy\nOptic atrophy is the damage or degeneration of the optic nerve, which can lead to vision loss.\n\nStep 3: Research each option\nA) Retinitis pigmentosa: Retinitis pigmentosa is a group of genetic disorders that affect the retina's ability to respond to light. It can lead to optic atrophy.\nB) Methanol poisoning: Methanol poisoning can cause optic nerve damage, leading to optic atrophy.\nC) Central retinal arterial occlusion (CRAO): CRAO is a blockage of the central retinal artery, which supplies blood to the retina. This can cause sudden vision loss and may lead to optic atrophy.\nD) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy: Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy is a condition that affects the blood vessels in the choroid, a layer of tissue at the back of the eye. It is characterized by abnormal blood vessels and polyp-like lesions. It does not directly cause optic atrophy.\n\nStep 4: Identify the correct answer\nBased on the research, the correct answer is D) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy, as it does not directly cause optic atrophy.",
