                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Understand the question\nThe question is asking which of the given options does not have optic atrophy as a symptom or result. Optic atrophy refers to the damage or degeneration of the optic nerve, which can lead to vision loss.\n\nStep 2: Review each option and determine if optic atrophy is associated with it\nA) Retinitis pigmentosa: This is a group of genetic disorders that affect the retina's ability to respond to light. It can cause optic atrophy.\nB) Methanol poisoning: Methanol poisoning can cause optic nerve damage, leading to optic atrophy.\nC) Central retinal arterial occlusion (CRAO): CRAO is a blockage of the central retinal artery, which supplies blood to the retina. This can cause damage to the retina and optic nerve, leading to optic atrophy.\nD) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy: This is a condition characterized by abnormal blood vessels in the choroid layer of the eye. It primarily affects the macula and does not typically cause optic atrophy.\n\nStep 3: Identify the option that does not have optic atrophy associated with it\nBased on the information above, the correct answer is D) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy, as it does not typically cause optic atrophy.",
                        "answers": [
