                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's rephrase the question to ensure we understand it correctly: Which of the following conditions does not involve optic atrophy?\n\nNow, let's work this out step by step by spelling out our thoughts and reasoning so anyone can verify them:\n\nA) Retinitis pigmentosa: This is a genetic disorder that affects the retina and can lead to vision loss. Optic atrophy, which is the degeneration of the optic nerve, can be seen in retinitis pigmentosa.\n\nB) Methanol poisoning: Methanol poisoning can cause optic nerve damage, leading to optic atrophy.\n\nC) Central retinal arterial occlusion (CRAO): CRAO is a condition where the central retinal artery is blocked, leading to a sudden loss of vision. Optic atrophy can occur as a result of the ischemic damage to the retina and optic nerve.\n\nD) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy: This is a condition that affects the blood vessels in the choroid layer of the eye, causing abnormal growth and leakage. Optic atrophy is not typically seen in polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy, as it primarily affects the choroidal vasculature and not the optic nerve.\n\nBased on our analysis, the correct answer is: D) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy.",
