                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I'm having trouble understanding why optic atrophy is not seen in one of these conditions. Can you help me work it out?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's go through each option step by step. First, let's discuss what optic atrophy is. Optic atrophy refers to the damage and loss of the optic nerve fibers, which can lead to vision loss. Now, let's look at each condition.\n\nStudent: Okay, let's start with option A, Retinitis pigmentosa.\n\nProfessor: Retinitis pigmentosa is a group of genetic disorders that affect the retina's ability to respond to light. It causes a slow and progressive loss of vision due to the degeneration of the photoreceptor cells. Optic atrophy can be seen in retinitis pigmentosa as the degeneration of the photoreceptor cells can lead to damage to the optic nerve fibers.\n\nStudent: I see. What about option B, Methanol poisoning?\n\nProfessor: Methanol poisoning can cause optic atrophy because methanol is toxic to the optic nerve. When ingested, methanol is metabolized to formaldehyde and formic acid, which can cause damage to the optic nerve fibers, leading to optic atrophy and vision loss.\n\nStudent: That makes sense. Now, what about option C, Central retinal arterial occlusion (CRAO)?\n\nProfessor: CRAO is a condition where the central retinal artery, which supplies blood to the retina, becomes blocked. This blockage can cause ischemia and damage to the retina, leading to vision loss. Optic atrophy can occur in CRAO as a result of the ischemic damage to the retina and the subsequent loss of the optic nerve fibers.\n\nStudent: Okay, I understand. Finally, what about option D, Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy?\n\nProfessor: Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy (PCV) is a condition characterized by abnormal blood vessels in the choroid, which is the layer of blood vessels and connective tissue between the retina and the sclera. These abnormal blood vessels can leak fluid and blood, causing damage to the retina. However, PCV does not directly affect the optic nerve fibers, and therefore, optic atrophy is not typically seen in this condition.\n\nStudent: So, the correct answer is option D, Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy, because it does not directly affect the optic nerve fibers and cause optic atrophy?\n\nProfessor: That's correct! Well done for working through each option and understanding",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "459257c0-f4d9-4f35-8c72-2b9428d888d5",
