                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Optic atrophy refers to the degeneration or damage to the optic nerve, which can lead to vision loss. It can be caused by various conditions that affect the optic nerve or the retina.\n\nA) Retinitis pigmentosa is a group of genetic disorders that affect the retina's ability to respond to light. It can cause optic atrophy as the disease progresses.\n\nB) Methanol poisoning can cause optic atrophy due to the toxic effect of methanol on the optic nerve.\n\nC) Central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO) is a condition where the blood flow to the retina is blocked, leading to ischemia and damage to the retina and optic nerve, which can result in optic atrophy.\n\nD) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy (PCV) is a condition characterized by abnormal blood vessels in the choroid layer of the eye, which can cause bleeding and fluid leakage under the retina. However, it does not directly affect the optic nerve and is not typically associated with optic atrophy.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy.",
