                    {
                        "id": "8471fe95-8871-42d3-ac68-848819c289ab",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The 5' to 3' direction refers to the direction in which nucleotides are added to a growing nucleic acid chain. In this direction, the 5' end of the nucleic acid chain has a phosphate group attached to the 5' carbon of the sugar molecule, while the 3' end has a hydroxyl group attached to the 3' carbon of the sugar molecule.\n\nA) DNA repair can occur in the 5' to 3' direction because it involves the removal and replacement of damaged nucleotides in a DNA strand, which can occur in either direction.\n\nB) DNA replication occurs in the 5' to 3' direction because new nucleotides are added to the 3' end of the growing DNA strand by DNA polymerase.\n\nC) RNA editing can occur in the 5' to 3' direction because it involves the modification of RNA molecules by adding, deleting, or changing nucleotides in the RNA sequence, which can occur in either direction.\n\nD) Transcription occurs in the 5' to 3' direction because RNA polymerase adds new nucleotides to the 3' end of the growing RNA strand, using the DNA template strand as a guide.\n\nTherefore, the answer is none of the above, as all of these processes can occur in the 5' to 3' direction.",
                        "answers": [
