                    {
                        "id": "fab8cb1b-61bc-4f06-a094-cc1f502d2135",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to understand what the 5' to 3' direction means and how it relates to each of the processes mentioned in the options.\n\nThe 5' to 3' direction refers to the orientation of the sugar-phosphate backbone in nucleic acids (DNA and RNA). The numbers 5' and 3' indicate the carbon atoms in the sugar molecule to which the phosphate groups are attached. In DNA and RNA synthesis, new nucleotides are added to the 3' end of the growing chain, resulting in the synthesis occurring in the 5' to 3' direction.\n\nNow let's analyze each option:\n\nA) DNA repair: DNA repair mechanisms, such as base excision repair, nucleotide excision repair, and mismatch repair, involve the removal of damaged or mismatched nucleotides and the synthesis of new DNA strands in the 5' to 3' direction. So, DNA repair occurs in the 5' to 3' direction.\n\nB) DNA replication: During DNA replication, new DNA strands are synthesized in the 5' to 3' direction. The leading strand is synthesized continuously, while the lagging strand is synthesized in short fragments called Okazaki fragments, which are later joined together. Both strands are synthesized in the 5' to 3' direction. So, DNA replication occurs in the 5' to 3' direction.\n\nC) RNA editing: RNA editing is a process that involves the modification of RNA molecules after transcription. This can include the insertion, deletion, or modification of individual nucleotides. RNA editing does not involve the synthesis of new RNA strands in the 5' to 3' direction. So, RNA editing doesn't occur in the 5' to 3' direction.\n\nD) Transcription: Transcription is the process of synthesizing RNA from a DNA template. During transcription, RNA polymerase synthesizes the RNA strand in the 5' to 3' direction. So, transcription occurs in the 5' to 3' direction.\n\nBased on the analysis, the correct answer is:\n\nC) RNA editing, as it doesn't occur in the 5' to 3' direction.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "4ca9f09d-d8a4-4bf2-812c-294e1d9090e6",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) RNA editing, as it doesn't occur in the 5' to 3' direction.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/04/14 18:42:31",
                        "api_service": "openai_chat",
