                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to understand the 5' to 3' direction and the processes involved in each of the given options. The 5' to 3' direction refers to the orientation of nucleotides in a DNA or RNA molecule. It is essential for various biological processes, such as DNA replication, transcription, and translation.\n\nA) DNA repair: DNA repair is the process of fixing damaged DNA molecules. It can occur in both 5' to 3' and 3' to 5' directions, depending on the type of repair mechanism involved. For example, base excision repair and nucleotide excision repair occur in the 5' to 3' direction.\n\nB) DNA replication: DNA replication is the process of copying a DNA molecule to produce two identical daughter molecules. It occurs in the 5' to 3' direction, as DNA polymerase can only add nucleotides to the 3' end of the growing DNA strand.\n\nC) RNA editing: RNA editing is the process of modifying RNA molecules after transcription. It can involve the insertion, deletion, or modification of nucleotides in the RNA molecule. RNA editing can occur in both 5' to 3' and 3' to 5' directions, depending on the type of editing involved.\n\nD) Transcription: Transcription is the process of synthesizing RNA from a DNA template. It occurs in the 5' to 3' direction, as RNA polymerase can only add nucleotides to the 3' end of the growing RNA strand.\n\nBased on this information, we can conclude that the process that doesn't exclusively occur in the 5' to 3' direction is:\n\nC) RNA editing",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "c3e801f4-2d10-466c-9030-05d75244e199",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) RNA editing.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/04/14 18:45:13",
                        "api_service": "openai_chat",
                        "model": "{'name': 'gpt-4', 'temperature': 0, 'max_tokens': 512}",
                        "comment": "",
                        "annotations": []
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "e5119f03-7f05-46bb-8187-68ad46e985b5",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I'm having trouble understanding the 5' to 3' direction concept. Can you help me figure out which of these processes doesn't occur in that direction?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's start by understanding what the 5' to 3' direction means. In nucleic acids like DNA and RNA, the 5' end refers to the end with a free phosphate group, while the 3' end has a free hydroxyl group. Most biological processes involving nucleic acids occur in the 5' to 3' direction. Now, let's go through each of the options one by one.\n\nStudent: Okay, let's start with DNA repair.\n\nProfessor: DNA repair is a process that fixes errors or damage in the DNA sequence. Most DNA repair mechanisms, like base excision repair and nucleotide excision repair, involve synthesis of new DNA strands in the 5' to 3' direction. So, DNA repair does occur in the 5' to 3' direction.\n\nStudent: Got it. What about DNA replication?\n\nProfessor: DNA replication is the process by which DNA is copied during cell division. The new DNA strand is synthesized in the 5' to 3' direction by DNA polymerase. So, DNA replication also occurs in the 5' to 3' direction.\n\nStudent: Okay, that makes sense. How about RNA editing?\n\nProfessor: RNA editing is a process that modifies RNA molecules after they have been transcribed from DNA. This can involve the insertion, deletion, or modification of individual nucleotides. RNA editing can occur in both the 5' to 3' and 3' to 5' directions, depending on the specific editing event. However, since it can occur in the 5' to 3' direction, it doesn't fit as the answer to your question.\n\nStudent: Alright, so that leaves transcription.\n\nProfessor: Yes, transcription is the process by which the information in a DNA sequence is copied into an RNA molecule. During transcription, RNA polymerase synthesizes the RNA strand in the 5' to 3' direction. So, transcription also occurs in the 5' to 3' direction.\n\nStudent: Wait, I'm confused. All of these processes seem to occur in the 5' to 3' direction. What's the right answer?\n\nProfessor: I apologize for the confusion earlier. I misspoke about RNA editing. While RNA editing can involve modifications in both directions, the actual process",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "d1e864d7-9aeb-4304-8041-70d497c14e8b",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "C) RNA editing, as it doesn't strictly occur in the 5' to 3' direction like the other processes.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "C",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
