                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, let's first understand what Tetralogy of Fallot is and how it affects a child's body.\n\nTetralogy of Fallot is a congenital heart defect that involves four anatomical abnormalities in the heart. These abnormalities affect the flow of blood through the heart and to the lungs, causing oxygen-poor blood to be pumped to the body. This results in episodes of cyanosis, or blue skin coloration, due to the lack of oxygen in the blood.\n\nNow, let's analyze each position and determine which one would be most beneficial for a child with Tetralogy of Fallot.\n\nA) Supine: This position involves lying flat on the back. In this position, blood flow to the lungs may not be optimal, and it may not help alleviate the symptoms of Tetralogy of Fallot.\n\nB) Prone: This position involves lying face down. Similar to the supine position, this position may not provide optimal blood flow to the lungs and may not help alleviate the symptoms of Tetralogy of Fallot.\n\nC) Squatting: Squatting is a position where the child bends their knees and lowers their body towards the ground. This position can help increase blood flow to the lungs by increasing the pressure in the lower body, which can help push more blood through the heart and into the lungs. This can help alleviate the symptoms of Tetralogy of Fallot.\n\nD) Leaning forwards: This position involves bending the upper body forward. While this position may provide some relief, it is not as effective as squatting in increasing blood flow to the lungs.\n\nBased on this analysis, the most beneficial position for a child with Tetralogy of Fallot is:\n\nC) Squatting",
