                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's rephrase the question to ensure we understand it correctly: Which position does a child with Tetralogy of Fallot typically use?\n\nNow, let's work this out step by step, spelling out our thoughts and reasoning so anyone can verify them:\n\nTetralogy of Fallot is a congenital heart defect that affects the structure of the heart, causing a combination of four related heart defects. These defects can lead to decreased oxygen in the blood, which can cause episodes of cyanosis (bluish discoloration of the skin) and shortness of breath.\n\nChildren with Tetralogy of Fallot may adopt certain positions to help alleviate their symptoms and increase blood flow to the lungs. Let's examine each of the given positions:\n\nA) Supine: Lying flat on the back. This position may not provide significant relief for a child with Tetralogy of Fallot, as it does not actively promote increased blood flow to the lungs.\n\nB) Prone: Lying face down. Similar to the supine position, this position may not provide significant relief for a child with Tetralogy of Fallot, as it does not actively promote increased blood flow to the lungs.\n\nC) Squatting: In this position, the child bends their knees and lowers their body towards the ground. Squatting can help increase blood flow to the lungs by increasing pressure in the lower extremities, which can help push more blood through the pulmonary circulation. This position is known to provide relief for children with Tetralogy of Fallot during episodes of cyanosis and shortness of breath.\n\nD) Leaning forwards: While this position may provide some relief by allowing the child to catch their breath, it does not actively promote increased blood flow to the lungs like squatting does.\n\nBased on this reasoning, the correct answer is:\n\nC) Squatting",
