                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Tetralogy of Fallot is a congenital heart defect that involves four anatomical abnormalities: ventricular septal defect, pulmonary stenosis, overriding aorta, and right ventricular hypertrophy. These abnormalities lead to decreased blood flow to the lungs and mixing of oxygen-poor blood with oxygen-rich blood, resulting in cyanosis (bluish discoloration of the skin due to low oxygen levels). \n\nTo answer the question of which position a child with tetralogy of Fallot uses, we need to consider which position helps alleviate their symptoms and improve blood flow.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. How does the supine position affect blood flow in a child with tetralogy of Fallot?\n2. How does the prone position affect blood flow in a child with tetralogy of Fallot?\n3. How does the squatting position affect blood flow in a child with tetralogy of Fallot?\n4. How does the leaning forward position affect blood flow in a child with tetralogy of Fallot?\n\n1. In the supine position (lying on the back), blood flow may not be significantly altered in a child with tetralogy of Fallot. This position does not provide any specific benefit in terms of improving blood flow or reducing cyanosis.\n\n2. In the prone position (lying on the stomach), blood flow may also not be significantly altered in a child with tetralogy of Fallot. Like the supine position, this position does not provide any specific benefit in terms of improving blood flow or reducing cyanosis.\n\n3. The squatting position can help improve blood flow in a child with tetralogy of Fallot. Squatting increases systemic vascular resistance, which reduces the amount of blood shunted from the right ventricle to the aorta, thereby increasing blood flow to the lungs and improving oxygenation. This position can help alleviate symptoms of cyanosis and shortness of breath.\n\n4. The leaning forward position may not provide significant benefits in terms of blood flow or symptom relief for a child with tetralogy of Fallot. This position does not specifically address the underlying anatomical abnormalities or improve blood flow to the lungs.\n\nBased on the analysis of the different positions, the answer is:\n\nC) Squatting\n\nA child with tetralogy of Fallot uses the squatting position to help improve blood flow, increase oxygenation, and alleviate symptoms of cyanosis and shortness of breath.",
