                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nTetralogy of Fallot is a congenital heart defect that involves four abnormalities in the heart's structure. These abnormalities include a ventricular septal defect (VSD), pulmonary stenosis, an overriding aorta, and right ventricular hypertrophy.\n\nThe child with tetralogy of Fallot experiences a decrease in blood flow to the lungs due to pulmonary stenosis. This results in reduced oxygenation of the blood, leading to cyanosis (bluish discoloration of the skin).\n\nTo improve oxygenation, the child may adopt a squatting position, which increases systemic vascular resistance and reduces the shunting of blood from the right ventricle to the left ventricle. This position helps to maintain a balance between pulmonary and systemic blood flow, thereby improving oxygenation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Squatting.",
