                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nD) Phosphorylase enzyme\n\nThis presentation is consistent with McArdle disease, which is caused by a deficiency in the enzyme phosphorylase. This enzyme is responsible for breaking down glycogen into glucose for energy use in muscle cells. Without it, glycogen accumulates in the muscle and causes pain and weakness during exercise. Muscle debranching enzyme deficiency (A) would result in a different type of glycogen storage disease, while phosphofructokinase I deficiency (B) would result in a different type of exercise intolerance. Glucose 6 phosphatase deficiency (C) would result in a different type of glycogen storage disease affecting the liver and kidneys.",
