                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Identify the condition associated with excessive glycogen in muscle tissue.\n2. Identify the enzymes involved in glycogen metabolism.\n3. Determine which enzyme deficiency is most likely to cause excessive glycogen in muscle tissue.\n\nAnswer:\n1. The condition associated with excessive glycogen in muscle tissue is glycogen storage disease.\n2. The enzymes involved in glycogen metabolism are muscle debranching enzyme, phosphofructokinase I, glucose 6 phosphatase, and phosphorylase enzyme.\n3. Based on the location of the excessive glycogen (muscle tissue) and the symptoms (pain in calf muscles on exercise), the most likely enzyme deficiency is muscle debranching enzyme (choice A). This deficiency leads to the accumulation of abnormal glycogen structures in muscle tissue, causing muscle weakness and pain during exercise.",
