                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The excessive amount of glycogen present in the muscle suggests a problem with glycogen metabolism. The fact that the pain occurs during exercise suggests that the muscle is not able to break down glycogen fast enough to meet the energy demands of exercise. This points to a deficiency in one of the enzymes involved in glycogen breakdown.\n\nOption A) Muscle debranching enzyme is involved in breaking down glycogen branches. Deficiency in this enzyme would lead to accumulation of glycogen with long branches, but this is not consistent with the presentation of the patient.\n\nOption B) Phosphofructokinase I is involved in the early steps of glycolysis, not glycogen breakdown. This is not a likely enzyme deficiency in this case.\n\nOption C) Glucose 6 phosphatase is involved in gluconeogenesis, not glycogen breakdown. This is not a likely enzyme deficiency in this case.\n\nOption D) Phosphorylase enzyme is the primary enzyme involved in glycogen breakdown. Deficiency in this enzyme would lead to accumulation of glycogen in the muscle, which is consistent with the presentation of the patient. Therefore, the most likely enzyme deficiency in this case is D) Phosphorylase enzyme.",
