                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Glycogen is a storage form of glucose in the body, primarily found in the liver and muscles. Enzymes are required for the breakdown and synthesis of glycogen.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the role of muscle debranching enzyme?\n2. What is the role of phosphofructokinase I?\n3. What is the role of glucose 6 phosphatase?\n4. What is the role of phosphorylase enzyme?\n5. Which enzyme deficiency is associated with excessive glycogen in muscle tissue?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Muscle debranching enzyme is responsible for breaking down the branches of glycogen during glycogenolysis.\n2. Phosphofructokinase I is an enzyme involved in the glycolytic pathway, which breaks down glucose to produce energy.\n3. Glucose 6 phosphatase is an enzyme found in the liver and kidneys that converts glucose-6-phosphate to glucose, allowing it to be released into the bloodstream.\n4. Phosphorylase enzyme is responsible for breaking down glycogen into glucose-1-phosphate during glycogenolysis.\n5. Excessive glycogen in muscle tissue is most likely due to a deficiency in muscle debranching enzyme, which prevents the breakdown of glycogen branches. Therefore, the most likely enzyme deficiency in this case is A) Muscle debranching enzyme.",
                        "answers": [
