                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to understand the role of each enzyme in glycogen metabolism and how their deficiency can lead to excessive glycogen accumulation in muscles.\n\nA) Muscle debranching enzyme: This enzyme is involved in the breakdown of glycogen by removing branches from the glycogen molecule. A deficiency in this enzyme can lead to a buildup of glycogen in muscles, causing a condition called glycogen storage disease type III (Cori disease).\n\nB) Phosphofructokinase I: This enzyme is involved in glycolysis, the process of breaking down glucose to produce energy. A deficiency in this enzyme can lead to a buildup of glycogen in muscles, causing a condition called glycogen storage disease type VII (Tarui disease).\n\nC) Glucose 6 phosphatase: This enzyme is involved in the final step of glycogenolysis, the process of breaking down glycogen to release glucose. A deficiency in this enzyme can lead to a buildup of glycogen in the liver and kidneys, causing a condition called glycogen storage disease type I (von Gierke disease). This enzyme deficiency does not typically cause excessive glycogen accumulation in muscles.\n\nD) Phosphorylase enzyme: This enzyme is involved in the initial step of glycogenolysis, the process of breaking down glycogen to release glucose. A deficiency in this enzyme can lead to a buildup of glycogen in muscles, causing a condition called glycogen storage disease type V (McArdle disease).\n\nNow, let's answer the sub-questions:\n\n1) Which enzyme deficiencies can cause excessive glycogen accumulation in muscles?\nAnswer: Muscle debranching enzyme, Phosphofructokinase I, and Phosphorylase enzyme.\n\n2) Which enzyme deficiency is most likely in an adolescent male patient with pain in calf muscles on exercise?\nAnswer: Phosphorylase enzyme deficiency, as it is the most common cause of exercise-induced muscle pain and weakness due to impaired glycogen breakdown in muscles (McArdle disease).\n\nTherefore, the most likely enzyme deficiency in this patient is D) Phosphorylase enzyme.",
