                    {
                        "id": "2efe48ae-d795-4d37-a0aa-27b475695a04",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nTo answer this question, we need to understand the role of glycogen in the body. Glycogen is a polysaccharide that is stored in the muscles and liver and is the body's main source of energy. When the body needs energy, glycogen is broken down into glucose, which is then used for energy.\n\nThe process of breaking down glycogen into glucose is called glycogenolysis. This process requires several enzymes, including muscle debranching enzyme, phosphofructokinase I, glucose 6 phosphatase, and phosphorylase enzyme.\n\nBased on the information provided in the question, the most likely enzyme deficiency is phosphorylase enzyme. This enzyme is responsible for breaking down glycogen into glucose, and an excessive amount of glycogen present in the muscle indicates that the enzyme is not functioning properly. Therefore, the most likely enzyme deficiency is phosphorylase enzyme (D).",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "48bdee24-2fe4-4c3b-a090-159ef2c48756",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": " D) Phosphorylase enzyme.",
                                "answer_from_choices": "D",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/04/17 14:55:43",
                        "api_service": "openai",
                        "model": "{'name': 'text-davinci-003', 'temperature': 0, 'max_tokens': 512}",
                        "comment": "",
                        "annotations": []
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "b65e7fa2-f06d-4d64-99fd-f06c112f4e4d",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nFirst, let's rephrase the question: What enzyme deficiency is most likely causing the excessive amount of glycogen present in the adolescent male patient's calf muscles after exercise?\n\nSecond, let's work this out step by step: \n\nGlycogen is a polysaccharide composed of glucose molecules. In order for glycogen to be stored in the muscle, glucose molecules must first be converted into glycogen. This process is known as glycogenesis and is catalyzed by the enzyme glycogen synthase. Therefore, an enzyme deficiency in glycogen synthase would result in an excessive amount of glycogen present in the muscle.\n\nThird, let's make sure we have the right answer:\n\nThe most likely enzyme deficiency causing the excessive amount of glycogen present in the adolescent male patient's calf muscles after exercise is D) Phosphorylase enzyme.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "43be4b87-4888-4ca2-a1fa-10c32fa1fb1c",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
