                    {
                        "id": "0d46cf91-0d42-43e8-8736-daa4de06566c",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nStudent: What is the most likely enzyme deficiency in this case?\n\nProfessor: Let's break it down. The patient has pain in the calf muscles when exercising and a biopsy revealed excessive amounts of glycogen. So, the most likely enzyme deficiency is related to glycogen metabolism. The four options you have are A) Muscle debranching enzyme, B) Phosphofructokinase I, C) Glucose 6 phosphatase, and D) Phosphorylase enzyme. \n\nStudent: What is the difference between these enzymes?\n\nProfessor: The muscle debranching enzyme is responsible for breaking down the glycogen molecule into smaller pieces. Phosphofructokinase I is an enzyme that helps to convert fructose-6-phosphate into fructose-1,6-bisphosphate. Glucose 6 phosphatase is an enzyme that helps to convert glucose-6-phosphate into glucose. Finally, phosphorylase enzyme helps to convert glycogen into glucose-1-phosphate. So, the most likely enzyme deficiency in this case is D) Phosphorylase enzyme.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "a937d8a9-19c6-4322-96e0-6af30e4aaca9",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
