                    {
                        "id": "be5b5fa0-7f1b-4e96-bbe9-e879328eacf5",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "refl-01",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) Muscle debranching enzyme\n\nCritique: The given information suggests that the patient is suffering from glycogen storage disease type III (also known as Cori disease), which is caused by a deficiency in the enzyme debranching enzyme. This deficiency leads to the accumulation of abnormal glycogen in the liver and muscles, resulting in muscle weakness and pain during exercise.\n\nReconsidering other answer options:\nB) Phosphofructokinase I deficiency causes glycogen storage disease type VII (also known as Tarui disease), which presents with exercise intolerance, muscle cramps, and myoglobinuria.\nC) Glucose 6 phosphatase deficiency causes glycogen storage disease type I (also known as von Gierke disease), which presents with hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, and hepatomegaly.\nD) Phosphorylase enzyme deficiency causes glycogen storage disease type V (also known as McArdle disease), which presents with exercise intolerance, muscle cramps, and myoglobinuria.\n\nFinal answer: A) Muscle debranching enzyme.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "e1e187d6-5cba-416c-9b2f-ff709b900caa",
