                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient is experiencing pain in calf muscles during exercise and has excessive glycogen in the muscle tissue. This suggests a problem with glycogen metabolism in the muscles.\n\nA) Muscle debranching enzyme deficiency would lead to a buildup of glycogen with abnormal branching, which is not mentioned in the question.\n\nB) Phosphofructokinase I deficiency would affect glycolysis, not glycogen metabolism.\n\nC) Glucose 6 phosphatase deficiency would lead to glycogen storage disease type I (von Gierke disease), which primarily affects the liver and kidneys, not the muscles.\n\nD) Phosphorylase enzyme deficiency would impair the breakdown of glycogen in the muscles, leading to a buildup of glycogen and muscle pain during exercise. This is consistent with the patient's symptoms and biopsy findings.\n\nTherefore, the most likely enzyme deficiency in this patient is D) Phosphorylase enzyme.",
