                    {
                        "id": "207296c5-522c-4251-9e1f-cd6c1a51a9c3",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "zhou-01-ins",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Understand the pivot shift test\nThe pivot shift test is a physical examination performed by a healthcare professional to assess the stability of the knee joint, specifically focusing on the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL).\n\nStep 2: Identify the function of each option\nA) Posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) - The PCL is responsible for preventing the tibia from sliding too far backward and provides stability to the knee joint.\nB) Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) - The ACL is responsible for preventing the tibia from sliding too far forward and provides rotational stability to the knee joint.\nC) Medial meniscus - The medial meniscus is a piece of cartilage that provides cushioning and stability to the knee joint.\nD) Lateral meniscus - The lateral meniscus is another piece of cartilage that provides cushioning and stability to the knee joint.\n\nStep 3: Determine which option is related to the pivot shift test\nSince the pivot shift test is specifically designed to assess the stability of the knee joint and focuses on the ACL, the correct answer is:\n\nB) Anterior cruciate ligament",
                        "answers": [
