                    {
                        "id": "bc5bd033-a8e7-40ef-9b81-8f91c19c8698",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To determine the correct answer, let's first understand what a positive pivot shift test is and what it indicates.\n\nA pivot shift test is a clinical test used by medical professionals to assess the stability of the knee joint, specifically the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL). The test is performed by flexing and extending the knee while applying a valgus (outward) force and internal rotation to the lower leg. A positive pivot shift test occurs when there is a sudden \"shift\" or \"clunk\" during the test, indicating instability in the knee joint.\n\nNow, let's analyze each of the given options:\n\nA) Posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) - The PCL is one of the major ligaments in the knee, but it is responsible for preventing the tibia (shin bone) from moving too far backward. A positive pivot shift test is not directly related to PCL injury.\n\nB) Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) - The ACL is another major ligament in the knee, and it is responsible for preventing the tibia from moving too far forward. A positive pivot shift test is an indication of ACL injury, as it demonstrates instability in the knee joint due to the compromised function of the ACL.\n\nC) Medial meniscus - The medial meniscus is a piece of cartilage in the knee that acts as a cushion between the femur (thigh bone) and tibia. While a medial meniscus injury can cause knee pain and instability, it is not the primary cause of a positive pivot shift test.\n\nD) Lateral meniscus - The lateral meniscus is another piece of cartilage in the knee, similar to the medial meniscus. Like the medial meniscus, injury to the lateral meniscus can cause knee pain and instability, but it is not the primary cause of a positive pivot shift test.\n\nBased on the information above, the correct answer is:\n\nB) Anterior cruciate ligament",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "0fe6e292-924e-4417-9a14-fed14a29d898",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) Anterior cruciate ligament",
                                "answer_from_choices": "B",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
