                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The positive pivot shift test in the knee is because of injury to the anterior cruciate ligament (B).\n\nCritique: The answer is correct and directly addresses the question. It identifies the specific ligament responsible for a positive pivot shift test in the knee.\n\nReconsidering other answer options:\nA) Posterior cruciate ligament - This ligament is not responsible for a positive pivot shift test. It is tested using the posterior drawer test.\nC) Medial meniscus - Injuries to the medial meniscus are typically assessed using the McMurray test, not the pivot shift test.\nD) Lateral meniscus - Similar to the medial meniscus, injuries to the lateral meniscus are assessed using the McMurray test, not the pivot shift test.\n\nFinal answer: The positive pivot shift test in the knee is because of injury to the anterior cruciate ligament (B).",
