                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "B) Anterior cruciate ligament\n\nCritique: The positive pivot shift test is a clinical test used to diagnose anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) injuries. During the test, the examiner applies a valgus force to the knee while it is flexed and then rotates the tibia. If the tibia subluxates anteriorly, it indicates a positive test and suggests an ACL injury. Therefore, option B is the correct answer.\n\nReconsidering other answer options: None of the other options are correct as they do not cause a positive pivot shift test.\n\nFinal answer: B) Anterior cruciate ligament.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
