                    {
                        "id": "82fc9eca-2217-452f-9821-e4f8e8e20707",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "All cells use cellular respiration to release energy.\n\nHelpful knowledge and important additional facts:\n- Cellular respiration is a process that occurs in the cells of organisms to convert biochemical energy from nutrients into adenosine triphosphate (ATP), which is then used to fuel cellular activities.\n- There are two types of cellular respiration: aerobic respiration (requires oxygen) and anaerobic respiration (does not require oxygen).\n- Photosynthesis is a process used by plants and some other organisms to convert light energy into chemical energy that can be later released to fuel the organisms' activities.\n- Meiosis is a type of cell division that results in four daughter cells, each with half the number of chromosomes of the parent cell, as in the production of gametes and plant spores.\n- Releasing waste is a process by which cells expel waste products and toxins from their interior to maintain a stable internal environment.\n\nSub-questions:\n1) Does cellular respiration involve photosynthesis?\nNo, cellular respiration and photosynthesis are two separate processes. Photosynthesis is used by plants and some other organisms to convert light energy into chemical energy, while cellular respiration is used by all cells to release energy from nutrients.\n\n2) Does cellular respiration involve releasing waste?\nWhile cellular respiration does produce waste products, such as carbon dioxide and water, its primary function is to release energy in the form of ATP. Therefore, releasing waste is not the main purpose of cellular respiration.\n\n3) Does cellular respiration involve meiosis?\nNo, cellular respiration and meiosis are two separate processes. Cellular respiration is involved in energy production, while meiosis is a type of cell division that results in the production of gametes and plant spores.\n\n4) Does cellular respiration involve releasing energy?\nYes, the primary function of cellular respiration is to release energy from nutrients in the form of ATP, which can then be used to fuel cellular activities.\n\nBased on the answers to the sub-questions, the original question can be answered: All cells use cellular respiration to release energy (option D).",
                        "answers": [
