                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: I'm not sure about the answer to this question. Can you help me understand how to figure out which product can be made from moving winds?\n\nProfessor: Of course! Let's start by looking at each option and discussing whether it's possible for moving winds to create that product. First, let's consider wood. Can moving winds create wood?\n\nStudent: No, I don't think so. Wood comes from trees.\n\nProfessor: That's correct. Now, let's look at bananas. Can moving winds create bananas?\n\nStudent: No, bananas grow on plants.\n\nProfessor: Right again. Now, let's consider electricity. Can moving winds create electricity?\n\nStudent: I'm not sure. How would that work?\n\nProfessor: Well, moving winds can be harnessed by wind turbines, which are machines that convert the kinetic energy from the wind into electrical energy. This electrical energy can then be used to power homes, businesses, and other things that require electricity. Does that make sense?\n\nStudent: Yes, I think so. So moving winds can create electricity through wind turbines.\n\nProfessor: Exactly! Now, let's look at the last option, metal. Can moving winds create metal?\n\nStudent: No, metal comes from the ground and is extracted through mining.\n\nProfessor: Correct! So, based on our discussion, which option do you think is the correct answer?\n\nStudent: I think the correct answer is C) electricity, because moving winds can be harnessed by wind turbines to create electrical energy.\n\nProfessor: That's right! Good job working through the options and understanding the process.",
                        "answers": [
