                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Worms are decomposers that play an important role in breaking down dead organic matter and returning nutrients to the soil.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the process by which worms return nutrients to the soil?\n2. What is regurgitation?\n3. What is decomposition?\n4. What is recycling?\n5. What is burial?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The process by which worms return nutrients to the soil is called decomposition. Worms eat dead organic matter and break it down into smaller pieces, which are then further broken down by bacteria and fungi. The nutrients from the dead matter are released into the soil, where they can be taken up by plants.\n2. Regurgitation is the process by which an animal brings up partially digested food from its stomach and into its mouth to be chewed again. This is not the process by which worms return nutrients to the soil.\n3. Decomposition is the correct answer. See answer to sub-question 1.\n4. Recycling is a general term that refers to the process of reusing materials or resources. While worms do play a role in recycling nutrients in the soil, it is not the specific term used to describe the process by which they return nutrients from dead organisms to the soil.\n5. Burial is not the correct answer. Burial refers to the act of placing a dead organism underground or in a tomb, which does not involve the process of returning nutrients to the soil.",
                        "answers": [
