                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "C) daughter\n\nTo use a Punnett square to calculate the chance of a trait being passed to someone's daughter, we need to know the genotype of the parents. Let's say the mother is homozygous dominant (AA) for a certain trait, and the father is heterozygous (Aa) for the same trait.\n\nWe can then create a Punnett square by writing the mother's alleles (AA) on the top row and the father's alleles (Aa) on the left column. We then fill in the boxes with the possible combinations of alleles that their offspring could inherit.\n\n|   | A | A |\n| - | - | - |\n| A | AA | AA |\n| a | Aa | Aa |\n\nFrom this Punnett square, we can see that there is a 100% chance that their daughter will inherit at least one dominant allele (Aa or AA), and a 50% chance that she will inherit two dominant alleles (AA). Therefore, the chance of the trait being passed to someone's daughter is 100%.",
