                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's rephrase the question to ensure we understand it correctly: Which family member's likelihood of inheriting a specific trait can be determined using a Punnett square?\n\nNow, let's work this out step by step, spelling out our thoughts and reasoning:\n\nA Punnett square is a tool used in genetics to predict the probability of offspring inheriting specific traits from their parents. It is based on the principles of Mendelian inheritance, which states that traits are determined by pairs of alleles (gene variants) inherited from each parent. \n\nA) Mother - A Punnett square cannot be used to calculate the chance of a trait being passed to someone's mother because the mother is the one passing the trait to her offspring, not the other way around.\n\nB) Grandfather - A Punnett square cannot be used to calculate the chance of a trait being passed to someone's grandfather because, like the mother, the grandfather is the one passing the trait to his offspring (the person's parent), not the other way around.\n\nC) Daughter - A Punnett square can be used to calculate the chance of a trait being passed to someone's daughter because it predicts the probability of offspring inheriting specific traits from their parents. In this case, the person and their partner would be the parents, and the daughter would be the offspring.\n\nD) Father - A Punnett square cannot be used to calculate the chance of a trait being passed to someone's father because, like the mother and grandfather, the father is the one passing the trait to his offspring, not the other way around.\n\nBased on our reasoning, the correct answer is:\n\nC) Daughter",
                        "answers": [
