                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Echolocation is a biological sonar system used by animals such as bats, dolphins, and whales to navigate and locate prey or obstacles in their environment. It works by emitting high-frequency sound waves and then listening for the echoes that bounce back.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What information can echolocation detect?\n2. How does echolocation work?\n3. What are the limitations of echolocation?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Echolocation can detect an object's distance, shape, and size.\n2. Echolocation works by emitting high-frequency sound waves that bounce off objects in the environment. The animal then listens for the echoes that bounce back and uses the timing and intensity of the echoes to determine the distance, shape, and size of the object.\n3. Echolocation has limitations in detecting certain types of objects, such as those that are too small or too far away to produce a detectable echo. It also cannot detect an object's temperature.",
