                        "cot": " \n\nFirst, let's look at the definition of echolocation: \n\n\"Echolocation is the ability of some animals to determine the direction and distance of objects by emitting sound waves and listening for the echoes.\"\n\nSo based on the definition, we can eliminate answers A, B, and C. This leaves us with D. \n\nNow let's think about how echolocation works. Animals that use echolocation emit sound waves, and then they listen for the echoes. The time it takes for the sound waves to bounce off the object and come back to the animal is how the animal determines the distance of the object. The direction of the object is determined by the direction the sound waves come back from. \n\nNowhere in this process is there any mention of temperature, so we can eliminate answer D, and our final answer is C.",
