                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To determine how many times someone would change the page of a calendar in a year, we need to consider how many months there are in a year.\n\nThere are 12 months in a year: January, February, March, April, May, June, July, August, September, October, November, and December.\n\nAt the beginning of each month, a person would change the page of the calendar to the new month. So, starting from January, they would change the page 11 times to reach December (since they don't need to change the page from December to January within the same year).\n\nHowever, we also need to consider the first time they change the page to start the year. This would be when they change the page from the previous year's December to the current year's January. This adds one more page change.\n\nSo, in total, someone would change the page of a calendar 11 times for the months within the year, plus 1 time for the initial change from the previous year's December to the current year's January.\n\n11 + 1 = 12\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) 12.",
