                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The brightness of a light source is measured in lumens. The distance between the light source and the observer affects how bright the light appears.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the distance between the observer and each light source?\n2. What is the brightness of each light source in lumens?\n\nAnswering sub-question 1:\n1. The distance between the observer and the light in the hall depends on where the observer is located in relation to the hall. \n2. The distance between the observer and the light in the room depends on where the observer is located in relation to the room. \n3. The distance between the observer and the star outside the window is extremely far, as stars are located in space. \n4. The distance between the observer and the streetlight outside the window depends on how close the streetlight is to the building.\n\nAnswering sub-question 2:\n1. The brightness of the light in the hall depends on the wattage of the light bulb and any other factors that may affect its brightness. \n2. The brightness of the light in the room depends on the wattage of the light bulb and any other factors that may affect its brightness. \n3. The brightness of the star outside the window depends on its distance from Earth and its luminosity. \n4. The brightness of the streetlight outside the window depends on the wattage of the light bulb and any other factors that may affect its brightness.\n\nConclusion:\nWithout knowing the specific distances and brightness levels of each light source, it is impossible to determine which light appears dimmest.",
