                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To answer this question, we need to consider the factors that affect the brightness of a light source as perceived by an observer. These factors include the intensity of the light source, the distance between the light source and the observer, and any obstructions or filters between the light source and the observer.\n\nSub-questions:\n1) What is the intensity of each light source?\n2) What is the distance between each light source and the observer?\n3) Are there any obstructions or filters between each light source and the observer?\n\nAnswers to sub-questions:\n1) We do not have specific information about the intensity of each light source. However, we can make some general assumptions. A star is likely to have the lowest intensity, followed by a streetlight, a light in the room, and the light in the hall.\n2) The distance between the observer and each light source is also not provided. However, we can assume that the light in the room is the closest, followed by the light in the hall, the streetlight, and the star.\n3) There may be obstructions or filters between the observer and some of the light sources. For example, the window may partially block or filter the light from the star and the streetlight. There may also be walls or doors between the observer and the light in the hall.\n\nBased on the answers to the sub-questions, we can conclude that the light that appears dimmest is likely to be the star outside the window (C). This is because the star has the lowest intensity, is the farthest away from the observer, and its light may be partially blocked or filtered by the window.",
